('cmr81vi4x00071y54fstoiqcb','["Topical corticosteroids", "Phototherapy", "Systemic immunosuppressants", "Coal tar preparations"]'::jsonb,'["Oral cyclosporine", "Topical corticosteroids", "Topical ketoconazole cream", "Narrowband UVB phototherapy", "Oral methotrexate"]'::jsonb,'Topical corticosteroids','Topical corticosteroids','B',md5('A 10-year-old boy in a pediatric clinic presents for a routine check-up. His mother reports that he has been experiencing itchy, scaly patches on his elbows and knees for the past few months. On examination, there are well-defined erythematous plaques with silvery scales. His BMI is 22 kg/m².'),'A 10-year-old boy in a pediatric clinic presents for a routine check-up. His mother reports that he has been experiencing itchy, scaly patches on his elbows and knees for the past few months. On examination, there are well-defined erythematous plaques with silvery scales. His BMI is 22 kg/m².',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('The correct answer is Topical corticosteroids, as they are the first-line treatment for mild to moderate psoriasis in children. They help reduce inflammation and scaling effectively. Option Phototherapy is incorrect because it is typically reserved for more severe cases of psoriasis that do not respond to topical treatments. Option Systemic immunosuppressants is incorrect because these are used for severe psoriasis and are not appropriate for a mild case. Option Coal tar preparations are less commonly used today and are not the first-line treatment, making them an incorrect choice in this scenario.'),'Limited plaque psoriasis in a child is treated first with a topical corticosteroid (AAD-NPF 2020 pediatric psoriasis guideline). Narrowband UVB, methotrexate and cyclosporine are for moderate-to-severe or refractory disease. Ketoconazole treats seborrheic dermatitis or tinea, not well-demarcated silvery plaques.'),
('cmr81vim5000c1y54pucssfy2','["Perimenopause", "Primary ovarian insufficiency", "Hyperthyroidism", "Cushing''s syndrome"]'::jsonb,'["Carcinoid syndrome", "Primary ovarian insufficiency", "Hyperthyroidism", "Perimenopause", "Pheochromocytoma"]'::jsonb,'Perimenopause','Perimenopause','D',md5('A 52-year-old woman in a surgical clinic presents with hot flashes, night sweats, and mood swings over the past 6 months. She has a history of hypertension and is currently taking lisinopril. Her vital signs are stable, and her BMI is 28. On examination, she has no palpable masses or abnormalities.'),'A 52-year-old woman in a primary care clinic presents with hot flashes, night sweats, and mood swings over the past 6 months. Her cycles have been irregular for the past year, and her last menstrual period was 3 months ago. She has a history of hypertension and is currently taking lisinopril. Her vital signs are stable, and her BMI is 28. On examination, she has no palpable masses or abnormalities.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The correct answer is perimenopause, as this condition is characterized by symptoms such as hot flashes, mood swings, and irregular menstrual cycles due to hormonal fluctuations. The patient''s age and symptoms align with this diagnosis. Option ''Primary ovarian insufficiency'' is incorrect because this condition typically presents in younger women, often under 40, with amenorrhea rather than perimenopausal symptoms. Option ''Hyperthyroidism'' is incorrect because it usually presents with additional symptoms such as weight loss, palpitations, and tremors, which are not reported here. Option ''Cushing''s syndrome'' is incorrect because it typically presents with features such as weight gain, hypertension, and skin changes, none of which are present in this vignette. Key takeaway: Perimenopause is a common diagnosis in women around this age presenting with vasomotor symptoms.'),'Age 52 with vasomotor symptoms, mood change and a year of irregular cycles (last period 3 months ago) fits perimenopause; menopause needs 12 months of amenorrhea (ACOG Practice Bulletin 141; NAMS 2022). Primary ovarian insufficiency is defined before age 40. Hyperthyroidism would add weight loss, tremor or tachycardia. Pheochromocytoma gives paroxysmal headache, palpitations and hypertension spikes; carcinoid flushing comes with diarrhea and wheeze.'),
('cmqgrcbzm00001yxd8eb4qzdl','["Orthostatic hypotension", "Hypertensive crisis", "Postural tachycardia syndrome", "Chronic fatigue syndrome"]'::jsonb,'["Orthostatic hypotension", "Carotid sinus hypersensitivity", "Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome", "Vasovagal syncope", "Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo"]'::jsonb,'Orthostatic hypotension','Orthostatic hypotension','A',md5('A 40-year-old male presents to the clinic for a follow-up visit after a recent diagnosis of hypertension. He is currently on lisinopril but reports occasional dizziness, especially when standing up quickly. His blood pressure is 130/85 mm Hg.'),'A 40-year-old male presents to the clinic for a follow-up visit after a recent diagnosis of hypertension. He is currently on lisinopril but reports occasional dizziness, especially when standing up quickly. Seated BP is 130/85 mm Hg with HR 78/min; after 3 minutes standing, BP is 106/70 mm Hg with HR 84/min.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms of dizziness upon standing, combined with his recent diagnosis of hypertension and current medication (lisinopril), suggest orthostatic hypotension. This condition occurs when blood pressure drops significantly upon standing, leading to symptoms like dizziness. A hypertensive crisis would present with significantly elevated blood pressure and acute symptoms. Postural tachycardia syndrome involves an increase in heart rate rather than a drop in blood pressure, and chronic fatigue syndrome does not typically present with these specific symptoms.'),'A fall of 24 mm Hg systolic within 3 minutes of standing meets the definition of orthostatic hypotension (systolic drop >=20 or diastolic >=10; AAS/EFAS consensus 2011), here likely from the ACE inhibitor. POTS needs an HR rise of >=30/min without hypotension. BPPV causes brief spinning vertigo with head turns, not lightheadedness on standing. Vasovagal syncope has a trigger and prodrome; carotid sinus hypersensitivity follows neck pressure or turning in older adults.'),
('cmqgdhx1r000e1yr87kzvb89z','["CT chest with contrast and tissue diagnosis planning", "Empiric antibiotics for 2 weeks", "Bronchodilator trial", "Reassurance and repeat X-ray in 6 months"]'::jsonb,'["Low-dose CT screening in 12 months", "CT chest with contrast and tissue diagnosis planning", "Empiric antibiotics and repeat chest X-ray in 6 weeks", "Sputum cytology and repeat chest X-ray in 3 months", "PET-CT and referral directly for lobectomy"]'::jsonb,'CT chest with contrast and tissue diagnosis planning','CT chest with contrast and tissue diagnosis planning','B',md5('A 55-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has a 2-month history of hemoptysis and 6-kg weight loss. BP 128/82 mm Hg, HR 88/min, SpO₂ 96% on room air. Chest X-ray shows a 3-cm right hilar mass.'),'A 55-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has a 2-month history of hemoptysis and 6-kg weight loss. BP 128/82 mm Hg, HR 88/min, SpO₂ 96% on room air. Chest X-ray shows a 3-cm right hilar mass.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?',md5('Hilar mass with hemoptysis and weight loss in a heavy smoker is lung cancer until proven otherwise — staging CT and biopsy pathway. Empiric antibiotics or observation delay diagnosis. Bronchodilators do not address the underlying mass.'),'A heavy smoker with hemoptysis, weight loss and a hilar mass has presumed lung cancer. The next step is a contrast CT of the chest (and upper abdomen) to stage the disease and pick the safest site for tissue diagnosis (ACCP lung cancer diagnosis guideline 2013; NCCN NSCLC 2025). Sputum cytology with a delayed X-ray, or empiric antibiotics, delay diagnosis. Surgery without tissue diagnosis and full staging is inappropriate. Screening LDCT is for asymptomatic people, not a symptomatic mass.'),
('cmqgdhvxc00041yr8s7oufad9','["Expectant management with weekly BP checks", "Start magnesium sulfate and plan delivery", "Prescribe lisinopril for blood pressure control", "Discharge with home BP monitoring"]'::jsonb,'["Start magnesium sulfate and continue pregnancy to 37 weeks", "Give betamethasone and discharge with home BP monitoring", "Expectant management with twice-weekly BP checks", "Start magnesium sulfate and plan delivery", "Start oral labetalol and repeat labs in 1 week"]'::jsonb,'Start magnesium sulfate and plan delivery','Start magnesium sulfate and plan delivery','D',md5('A 32-year-old G2P1 at 34 weeks gestation presents with BP 158/104 mm Hg on two readings 4 hours apart, 2+ proteinuria on dipstick, and a persistent headache. Reflexes are brisk. AST 28 U/L, ALT 32 U/L, platelets 198 × 10³/µL.'),'A 32-year-old G2P1 at 34 weeks gestation presents with BP 158/104 mm Hg on two readings 4 hours apart, 2+ proteinuria on dipstick, and a persistent headache. Reflexes are brisk. AST 28 U/L, ALT 32 U/L, platelets 198 × 10³/µL.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Preeclampsia with severe features (BP ≥160/110, symptoms) at ≥34 weeks warrants magnesium for seizure prophylaxis and delivery planning. ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy. Outpatient management is unsafe with severe features.'),'Preeclampsia with severe features (BP ≥160/110, symptoms) at ≥34 weeks warrants magnesium for seizure prophylaxis and delivery planning. ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy. Outpatient management is unsafe with severe features.

Why not "Expectant management with twice-weekly BP checks": a persistent headache is a severe feature; at >=34 weeks with severe features, delivery is indicated (ACOG Practice Bulletin 222).
Why not "Start oral labetalol and repeat labs in 1 week": antihypertensives do not treat the seizure risk or the indication for delivery.
Why not "Start magnesium sulfate and continue pregnancy to 37 weeks": expectant management to 37 weeks is for preeclampsia without severe features.
Why not "Give betamethasone and discharge with home BP monitoring": steroids are for <34 weeks, and severe features require inpatient care.'),
('cmqgdhzv700181yr8vn2g56vb','["Hold ACE inhibitor and evaluate for prerenal vs intrinsic cause", "Increase lisinopril dose", "Immediate dialysis", "Add spironolactone"]'::jsonb,'["Hold lisinopril and recheck potassium in 1 week", "Give IV calcium, hold lisinopril, and assess volume status", "Switch lisinopril to losartan and recheck potassium in 1 week", "Start sodium polystyrene and continue lisinopril", "Arrange urgent hemodialysis as the first step"]'::jsonb,'Hold ACE inhibitor and evaluate for prerenal vs intrinsic cause','Give IV calcium, hold lisinopril, and assess volume status','B',md5('A 72-year-old man with CKD stage 4 (baseline creatinine 2.8 mg/dL) is started on lisinopril and develops oliguria. Creatinine rises to 5.1 mg/dL, K 6.1 mEq/L. BP 102/64 mm Hg, HR 92/min.'),'A 72-year-old man with CKD stage 4 (baseline creatinine 2.8 mg/dL) is started on lisinopril and develops oliguria. Creatinine rises to 5.1 mg/dL, K 6.1 mEq/L. ECG shows peaked T waves. BP 102/64 mm Hg, HR 92/min.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step?'),'What is the most appropriate next step?',md5('Acute rise in creatinine after ACEi in advanced CKD may reflect hemodynamic AKI — hold offending agent and assess volume status. Increasing ACEi or adding MRA worsens hyperkalemia and renal function. Dialysis is not automatic without refractory complications.'),'ACE inhibitor-associated AKI with K 6.1 and peaked T waves needs IV calcium for membrane stabilization now, stopping the ACE inhibitor, and a volume assessment (UK Kidney Association hyperkalaemia guideline 2023; KDIGO AKI 2012). Then shift potassium (insulin-glucose) and remove it. Rechecking in a week ignores ECG changes. Dialysis is for refractory hyperkalemia or other urgent indications, not first. Losartan carries the same hyperkalemia and AKI risk. Sodium polystyrene is slow, and continuing lisinopril keeps the cause going.'),
('cmr828cjo00501y8w5v35zr1g','["Hypertensive emergency", "Migraine headache", "Tension-type headache", "Pseudotumor cerebri"]'::jsonb,'["Pseudotumor cerebri", "Migraine headache", "Hypertensive emergency", "Central retinal vein occlusion", "Hypertensive urgency"]'::jsonb,'Hypertensive emergency','Hypertensive emergency','C',md5('A 58-year-old woman in a primary care clinic presents with a persistent headache and blurred vision for the past week. She has a history of hypertension and is currently taking lisinopril. Upon examination, her blood pressure is 190/110 mm Hg, and she has a fundoscopic exam revealing retinal hemorrhages.'),'A 58-year-old woman in a primary care clinic presents with a persistent headache and blurred vision for the past week. She has a history of hypertension and is currently taking lisinopril. Upon examination, her blood pressure is 190/110 mm Hg, and she has a fundoscopic exam revealing retinal hemorrhages.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The correct answer is hypertensive emergency, as the patient presents with severely elevated blood pressure and signs of end-organ damage, such as retinal hemorrhages. This condition requires immediate management to lower blood pressure and prevent further complications. Option ''Migraine headache'' is incorrect because migraines typically do not present with such high blood pressure or retinal findings. Option ''Tension-type headache'' is incorrect because it usually does not cause significant visual changes or hypertension of this magnitude. Option ''Pseudotumor cerebri'' is incorrect as it generally presents with symptoms of increased intracranial pressure without the severe hypertension seen in this case.'),'BP 190/110 with headache, blurred vision and retinal hemorrhages means acute target-organ damage, so this is a hypertensive emergency (2017 ACC/AHA guideline). Hypertensive urgency is severe BP without organ damage. Migraine and pseudotumor do not explain the BP and fundoscopy. Central retinal vein occlusion is unilateral, with diffuse ''blood and thunder'' hemorrhages and no headache syndrome. Why not "Hypertensive urgency": the retinal hemorrhages are target-organ damage.'),
('cmqwuk5sz00151yzejbgq0p2j','["Immune-mediated inflammation", "Infectious etiology", "Ischemic damage", "Genetic mutation"]'::jsonb,'["Chronic mesenteric ischemic injury", "Immune-mediated inflammation", "Drug-induced injury", "Mycobacterial infection", "Eosinophilic infiltration"]'::jsonb,'Immune-mediated inflammation','Immune-mediated inflammation','B',md5('A 35-year-old female patient presents to the surgical clinic with abdominal pain and diarrhea. She reports that her symptoms have worsened over the past month. A colonoscopy reveals ulcerations in the terminal ileum. Biopsy shows granulomas.'),'A 35-year-old female patient presents to the gastroenterology clinic with abdominal pain and diarrhea. She reports that her symptoms have worsened over the past month. A colonoscopy reveals ulcerations in the terminal ileum. Biopsy shows granulomas.',md5('What is the most likely mechanism underlying this patient''s condition?'),'What is the most likely mechanism underlying this patient''s condition?',md5('The correct answer is immune-mediated inflammation, as the presence of granulomas and ulcerations in the terminal ileum is characteristic of Crohn''s disease, which is driven by an inappropriate immune response. Option infectious etiology is incorrect because the biopsy findings do not support an infectious process. Option ischemic damage is incorrect as it typically presents with different clinical features and is not associated with granulomas. Option genetic mutation is incorrect because while genetics may play a role in susceptibility, the immediate mechanism involves immune-mediated processes. Key takeaway: Crohn''s disease is characterized by immune-mediated inflammation leading to gastrointestinal symptoms.'),'Terminal ileal ulceration with noncaseating granulomas is Crohn disease, a dysregulated immune-mediated inflammation of the gut mucosa (ACG Crohn disease guideline 2025). Intestinal TB can also cause ileal granulomas, but they are caseating with AFB and usually an exposure history. Chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial pain without granulomas. Drug-induced (NSAID) injury gives ulcers or diaphragms, not granulomas. Eosinophilic infiltration describes eosinophilic gastroenteritis.'),
('cmqwuviet00gv1yzey62zuc1i','["Insulin deficiency", "Insulin resistance", "Increased glucagon secretion", "Decreased renal glucose threshold"]'::jsonb,'["Excess cortisol secretion", "Insulin deficiency", "Decreased renal glucose threshold", "Insulin resistance", "Increased glucagon secretion"]'::jsonb,'Insulin deficiency','Insulin deficiency','B',md5('A 35-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents to the clinic with complaints of polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss over the past week. She reports that she has been feeling fatigued and has had abdominal pain. Laboratory tests reveal a blood glucose level of 450 mg/dL and a serum bicarbonate level of 15 mEq/L.'),'A 35-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents to the clinic with complaints of polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss over the past week. She reports that she has been feeling fatigued and has had abdominal pain. Laboratory tests reveal a blood glucose level of 450 mg/dL and a serum bicarbonate level of 15 mEq/L.',md5('What is the most likely mechanism?'),'What is the most likely mechanism?',md5('The correct answer is insulin deficiency. In type 1 diabetes, the autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells leads to a lack of insulin production, resulting in hyperglycemia and the symptoms presented. The elevated blood glucose and low bicarbonate level suggest diabetic ketoacidosis, which is primarily due to insulin deficiency. Option ''Insulin resistance'' is incorrect because it is more characteristic of type 2 diabetes, where insulin is present but ineffective. Option ''Increased glucagon secretion'' is incorrect as glucagon levels are often elevated in the absence of insulin, but it is a consequence rather than the primary mechanism. Option ''Decreased renal glucose threshold'' is incorrect because while this occurs in diabetes, it is not the primary mechanism leading to the symptoms and hyperglycemia.'),'The correct answer is insulin deficiency. In type 1 diabetes, the autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells leads to a lack of insulin production, resulting in hyperglycemia and the symptoms presented. The elevated blood glucose and low bicarbonate level suggest diabetic ketoacidosis, which is primarily due to insulin deficiency. Option ''Insulin resistance'' is incorrect because it is more characteristic of type 2 diabetes, where insulin is present but ineffective. Option ''Increased glucagon secretion'' is incorrect as glucagon levels are often elevated in the absence of insulin, but it is a consequence rather than the primary mechanism. Option ''Decreased renal glucose threshold'' is incorrect because while this occurs in diabetes, it is not the primary mechanism leading to the symptoms and hyperglycemia.

Why not "Excess cortisol secretion": There are no Cushing features; marked hyperglycemia with low bicarbonate reflects insulin deficiency.'),
('cmqwuvohk00i01yzeezvjnsb5','["Colonoscopy", "Repeat fecal occult blood test", "CT scan of the abdomen", "Start a high-fiber diet"]'::jsonb,'["Repeat fecal occult blood test", "Multitarget stool DNA test", "Colonoscopy", "Flexible sigmoidoscopy", "CT colonography"]'::jsonb,'Colonoscopy','Colonoscopy','C',md5('A 40-year-old man presents to the primary care clinic for routine screening. He is asymptomatic but has a family history of colorectal cancer. A fecal occult blood test is positive. He has no significant past medical history and is up to date on vaccinations.'),'A 40-year-old man presents to the primary care clinic for routine screening. He is asymptomatic but has a family history of colorectal cancer. A fecal occult blood test is positive. He has no significant past medical history and is up to date on vaccinations.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('The correct answer is colonoscopy, which is the appropriate next step following a positive fecal occult blood test, especially in a patient with a family history of colorectal cancer. This allows for direct visualization and potential biopsy of any lesions. Option repeat fecal occult blood test is incorrect because it does not address the positive result and the need for further investigation. Option CT scan of the abdomen is incorrect as it is not the standard follow-up for a positive fecal occult blood test. Option starting a high-fiber diet is incorrect because while it is beneficial for overall colon health, it does not address the immediate concern of a positive screening test. The key takeaway is that a positive fecal occult blood test necessitates further evaluation with colonoscopy.'),'A positive stool test must be followed by colonoscopy (USMSTF 2017; ACG 2021 CRC screening). A family history of colorectal cancer also makes colonoscopy the screening test of choice from age 40. Repeating the FOBT or switching to a stool DNA test is wrong after a positive result. CT colonography and flexible sigmoidoscopy are screening options; they do not replace diagnostic colonoscopy after a positive test, and sigmoidoscopy misses proximal lesions.'),
('cmqwuwzlu00jq1yze7u83w7se','["Acute psychotic episode due to substance use", "Withdrawal from alcohol", "Schizophrenia", "Severe anxiety disorder"]'::jsonb,'["Substance-induced psychotic disorder", "First episode of schizophrenia", "Serotonin syndrome from a serotonergic drug", "Delirium due to alcohol withdrawal", "Brief psychotic disorder triggered by acute stress"]'::jsonb,'Acute psychotic episode due to substance use','Substance-induced psychotic disorder','A',md5('A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with acute onset of paranoia and hallucinations after using a new recreational drug at a party. He has a history of anxiety and is not currently on any medications. His vital signs are stable, but he appears agitated and is sweating profusely.'),'A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with acute onset of paranoia and hallucinations after smoking a synthetic cannabinoid at a party. He has a history of anxiety and is not currently on any medications. His vital signs are stable, (T 37.4°C); he is agitated and diaphoretic, with normal pupils, no clonus or hyperreflexia, and a normal glucose.',md5('What is the best explanation for the findings?'),'What is the best explanation for the findings?',md5('The correct answer is acute psychotic episode due to substance use, as the patient''s sudden onset of paranoia and hallucinations following recreational drug use is characteristic of substance-induced psychosis. This condition can occur with various substances, particularly stimulants. Option Withdrawal from alcohol is incorrect because withdrawal typically presents with tremors, seizures, and autonomic instability, not acute psychosis. Option Schizophrenia is incorrect as it is a chronic condition that does not present acutely in this manner. Option Severe anxiety disorder is incorrect because while anxiety can cause distress, it does not typically lead to hallucinations or paranoia.'),'Substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder (DSM-5-TR) fits psychosis that starts during intoxication with a synthetic cannabinoid. Why not: there is no clonus, hyperthermia or hyperreflexia, so this is not serotonin syndrome (Hunter criteria). Schizophrenia needs at least 6 months of symptoms. Brief psychotic disorder excludes substance causes. He has no alcohol-withdrawal history or timing.'),
('cmqghspl6000g1y91p8e7z8y0','["Ankle sprain", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Fracture", "Tendinitis"]'::jsonb,'["Ankle sprain", "Fracture", "Gout flare", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Tendinitis"]'::jsonb,'Ankle sprain','Ankle sprain','A',md5('A 30-year-old woman presents with severe pain in her left ankle after a fall while playing basketball. Physical examination reveals swelling, tenderness, and limited range of motion. An X-ray shows no fracture.'),'A 30-year-old woman presents with severe pain in her left ankle after a fall while playing basketball. Physical examination reveals swelling, tenderness, and limited range of motion. An X-ray shows no fracture.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The symptoms of swelling, tenderness, and limited range of motion after an injury, along with a negative X-ray, are indicative of an ankle sprain.

Clinical reasoning: Ankle sprains are common in sports injuries and can occur without visible fractures on X-ray.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Achilles tendon rupture: Typically presents with a sudden ''pop'' and inability to plantar flex the foot, which is not described here.
• Fracture: The X-ray shows no fracture, making this option unlikely.
• Tendinitis: Usually presents with pain that is more chronic and associated with overuse, rather than acute trauma.'),'The symptoms of swelling, tenderness, and limited range of motion after an injury, along with a negative X-ray, are indicative of an ankle sprain.

Clinical reasoning: Ankle sprains are common in sports injuries and can occur without visible fractures on X-ray.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Achilles tendon rupture: Typically presents with a sudden ''pop'' and inability to plantar flex the foot, which is not described here.
• Fracture: The X-ray shows no fracture, making this option unlikely.
• Tendinitis: Usually presents with pain that is more chronic and associated with overuse, rather than acute trauma.

Why not "Gout flare": Onset is traumatic, during sport, in a young woman with no history of gout.'),
('cmqghsvpv000v1y91x5mmbshd','["Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus", "Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY)"]'::jsonb,'["Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY)", "Prediabetes", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus"]'::jsonb,'Type 2 diabetes mellitus','Type 2 diabetes mellitus','C',md5('A 10-year-old girl presents to the clinic for a routine check-up. Her mother reports that she has been drinking excessive amounts of water and urinating frequently. The girl has a history of obesity and her BMI is in the 95th percentile. Laboratory tests reveal a fasting blood glucose level of 130 mg/dL.'),'A 10-year-old girl presents to the clinic for a routine check-up. Her mother reports that she has been drinking excessive amounts of water and urinating frequently. The girl has a history of obesity and her BMI is in the 95th percentile. Laboratory tests reveal a fasting blood glucose level of 130 mg/dL, confirmed at 132 mg/dL on repeat, and an HbA1c of 7.1%. She has acanthosis nigricans, urine ketones are negative, and islet autoantibodies are negative.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The presentation of polyuria, polydipsia, and elevated fasting blood glucose in a child with obesity is consistent with type 2 diabetes mellitus. This condition is increasingly diagnosed in pediatric populations due to rising obesity rates.

Clinical reasoning: The girl''s symptoms and laboratory findings suggest a metabolic disorder related to insulin resistance, typical of type 2 diabetes, rather than type 1 diabetes, which usually presents with more acute symptoms.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Type 1 diabetes mellitus: This typically presents with acute symptoms such as weight loss and ketoacidosis, which are not described in this vignette.
• Diabetes insipidus: This condition is characterized by polyuria due to a deficiency of antidiuretic hormone, not hyperglycemia.
• Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY): MODY is a genetic form of diabetes that typically presents in adolescents or young adults, and the clinical features here align more closely with type 2 diabetes.'),'Two fasting glucose values >=126 mg/dL and an HbA1c >=6.5% confirm diabetes (ADA Standards of Care 2026). Obesity, acanthosis nigricans, no ketones and negative islet autoantibodies make it type 2. Type 1 is unlikely with negative autoantibodies. MODY is suspected in non-obese, antibody-negative youth with a strong multigenerational family history. Prediabetes is HbA1c 5.7-6.4% or a fasting glucose of 100-125 mg/dL. Diabetes insipidus causes polyuria without hyperglycemia.'),
('cmqgjpl4f001m1y4vw0uqmbvc','["Administer calcium gluconate", "Initiate insulin therapy", "Increase dialysis frequency", "Start potassium binders"]'::jsonb,'["Administer IV furosemide", "Administer calcium gluconate", "Give IV insulin with dextrose", "Arrange urgent hemodialysis", "Start oral patiromer"]'::jsonb,'Administer calcium gluconate','Administer calcium gluconate','B',md5('A 55-year-old female with chronic kidney disease presents for a routine follow-up. She is currently on hemodialysis three times a week. Recent lab results show a serum potassium level of 6.5 mEq/L. She reports muscle weakness and palpitations.'),'A 55-year-old female with chronic kidney disease presents for a routine follow-up. She is currently on hemodialysis three times a week. Recent lab results show a serum potassium level of 6.5 mEq/L. She reports muscle weakness and palpitations. ECG shows peaked T waves.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('In the setting of hyperkalemia with ECG changes or significant symptoms, calcium gluconate is indicated to stabilize the cardiac membrane.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms of muscle weakness and palpitations, along with the elevated potassium level, necessitate immediate intervention to protect the heart.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Initiate insulin therapy: While insulin can help lower serum potassium, it does not provide immediate cardiac protection like calcium gluconate.
• Increase dialysis frequency: While this may be necessary, it is not an immediate action for symptomatic hyperkalemia.
• Start potassium binders: Potassium binders can help manage hyperkalemia but are not the first-line treatment in acute situations.'),'Hyperkalemia with ECG changes is treated first with IV calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane (UK Kidney Association 2023; KDIGO 2020 controversies conference). Insulin-dextrose shifts potassium and follows calcium. Urgent dialysis removes potassium and comes after stabilization. Patiromer is slow-acting. Furosemide is ineffective in a patient on hemodialysis.'),
('cmqgquzto000b1y4vkacfpb7z','["Angioedema", "ACE inhibitor-induced cough", "Hypertensive crisis", "Secondary hypertension"]'::jsonb,'["Gastroesophageal reflux-related cough", "Angioedema", "Cough-variant asthma", "Upper airway cough syndrome", "ACE inhibitor-induced cough"]'::jsonb,'ACE inhibitor-induced cough','ACE inhibitor-induced cough','E',md5('A 40-year-old man presents to the clinic with concerns about a recent diagnosis of hypertension. He is currently taking lisinopril but is experiencing persistent dry cough. He has no other significant medical history. His blood pressure is well-controlled at 120/80 mm Hg.'),'A 40-year-old man presents to the clinic with concerns about a recent diagnosis of hypertension. He is currently taking lisinopril but is experiencing persistent dry cough. He has no other significant medical history. His blood pressure is well-controlled at 120/80 mm Hg.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s persistent dry cough is a known side effect of ACE inhibitors like lisinopril.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette indicates the temporal relationship between the initiation of lisinopril and the onset of cough, supporting the diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Angioedema: Angioedema is a more severe reaction and is typically associated with swelling rather than cough.
• Hypertensive crisis: The patient’s blood pressure is well-controlled, making this diagnosis unlikely.
• Secondary hypertension: There is no indication of an underlying cause for hypertension in this vignette.'),'A dry cough in a patient on lisinopril with controlled BP is the classic ACE inhibitor cough (bradykinin accumulation); switching to an ARB resolves it (2017 ACC/AHA; ACCP 2006 cough guideline). Angioedema is swelling, not cough. GERD-related cough, upper airway cough syndrome and cough-variant asthma are common cough causes, but nothing in the stem (heartburn, postnasal drip, wheeze) points to them, and the drug is the obvious culprit.'),
('cmqgrep1g000a1yxdmc0xrzuy','["Herniated disc", "Muscle strain", "Spinal stenosis", "Spondylolisthesis"]'::jsonb,'["Spinal stenosis", "Muscle strain", "Spondylolisthesis", "Spinal abscess", "Herniated disc"]'::jsonb,'Herniated disc','Herniated disc','E',md5('A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe lower back pain after lifting a heavy object. He reports numbness in his left leg and difficulty walking. On examination, he has decreased reflexes in the left lower extremity.'),'A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe lower back pain after lifting a heavy object. He reports numbness in his left leg and difficulty walking. On examination, he has decreased reflexes in the left lower extremity.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The vignette describes a young man with acute lower back pain following heavy lifting, accompanied by neurological symptoms such as numbness and decreased reflexes in the left leg. These findings are characteristic of a herniated disc, where the intervertebral disc protrudes and compresses nearby nerve roots, leading to radicular symptoms. Muscle strain typically does not present with neurological deficits. Spinal stenosis and spondylolisthesis are less likely given the acute nature of the presentation and specific symptoms.'),'The vignette describes a young man with acute lower back pain following heavy lifting, accompanied by neurological symptoms such as numbness and decreased reflexes in the left leg. These findings are characteristic of a herniated disc, where the intervertebral disc protrudes and compresses nearby nerve roots, leading to radicular symptoms. Muscle strain typically does not present with neurological deficits. Spinal stenosis and spondylolisthesis are less likely given the acute nature of the presentation and specific symptoms.

Why not "Spinal abscess": There is no fever, injection drug use or other risk factor, and onset is acute after lifting.'),
('cmqgssfrz000k1yy7qr6o846k','["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Nephrotic syndrome", "Acute kidney injury", "Diabetes insipidus"]'::jsonb,'["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Nephrotic syndrome", "Acute tubular necrosis from dehydration", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome", "IgA nephropathy"]'::jsonb,'Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis','Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis','A',md5('A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic with a 2-day history of decreased urine output and swelling in his legs and face. His mother reports he had a sore throat a week ago, and his blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows hematuria and proteinuria.'),'A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic with a 2-day history of decreased urine output and swelling in his legs and face. His mother reports he had a sore throat a week ago, and his blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows hematuria and proteinuria.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The vignette describes a child with recent pharyngitis followed by symptoms of edema and decreased urine output, which are classic features of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN). PSGN typically presents 1-3 weeks after a streptococcal infection. The presence of hematuria and proteinuria on urinalysis further supports this diagnosis. Nephrotic syndrome is less likely due to the absence of significant proteinuria and the child''s recent history of infection. Acute kidney injury could explain decreased urine output but does not account for the recent infection or the edema. Diabetes insipidus typically presents with polyuria and polydipsia, not edema.'),'Post-streptococcal GN follows pharyngitis by 1-3 weeks with edema, hematuria and HTN (KDIGO 2021 GN guideline). Why not: IgA nephropathy causes hematuria at the same time as the URI (synpharyngitic), not a week later. Nephrotic syndrome lacks hematuria. HUS needs diarrhea, anemia and thrombocytopenia. ATN does not explain hematuria with edema and HTN after pharyngitis.'),
('cmqgz35ti003b1ybcxaibpre0','["Measure a D-dimer level to confirm the diagnosis", "Assess right ventricular function (CT or echocardiography) and measure cardiac troponin", "Discharge home on a direct oral anticoagulant without further testing", "Administer systemic thrombolysis because her heart rate is above 110/min"]'::jsonb,'["Give systemic thrombolysis because her heart rate is above 110/min", "Discharge home today on a direct oral anticoagulant since BP is normal", "Place an inferior vena cava filter before starting anticoagulation", "Obtain leg duplex ultrasound and anticoagulate if a DVT is found", "Assess right ventricular function and measure cardiac troponin"]'::jsonb,'Assess right ventricular function (CT or echocardiography) and measure cardiac troponin','Assess right ventricular function and measure cardiac troponin','E',md5('A 65-year-old woman is diagnosed with an acute pulmonary embolism on CT pulmonary angiography. She is alert. BP 132/78 mm Hg, HR 112/min, SpO2 91% on room air (96% on 2 L nasal cannula). She has no history of cancer or chronic cardiopulmonary disease. Her simplified PESI score is 1 (heart rate ≥110).'),'A 65-year-old woman is diagnosed with an acute pulmonary embolism on CT pulmonary angiography. She is alert. BP 132/78 mm Hg, HR 112/min, SpO2 91% on room air (96% on 2 L nasal cannula). She has no history of cancer or chronic cardiopulmonary disease. Her simplified PESI score is 1 (heart rate ≥110).',md5('Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to guide her management?'),'Which of the following is the most appropriate next step to guide her management?',md5('In normotensive PE with sPESI ≥1, ESC 2019 recommends risk stratification with RV assessment (echo or CT) and troponin to separate intermediate-high from intermediate-low risk. That determines the level of monitoring and rescue planning. Thrombolysis is not indicated without hemodynamic decompensation; tachycardia alone is not instability. Early discharge is for low-risk PE (sPESI 0), and she is hypoxemic. D-dimer only helps rule out PE in low-probability patients and adds nothing after a positive CTPA. Takeaway: normotensive PE -> sPESI, then RV imaging and troponin, then anticoagulate.'),'With a simplified PESI of 1 the patient is not low risk, so the next step is to check RV function (CT or echo) and troponin to separate intermediate-low from intermediate-high risk (ESC 2019 PE guideline). Early discharge is only for sPESI 0. Thrombolysis is for hemodynamic instability (high risk), not tachycardia. An IVC filter is for patients who cannot be anticoagulated. Leg ultrasound adds nothing once the PE is confirmed: she needs anticoagulation either way.'),
('cmqgdhxfb000i1yr8ob6juadu','["Supportive care and counseling on transmission", "Immediate antiviral therapy with tenofovir", "Liver biopsy before treatment", "Isolation with ribavirin prophylaxis for contacts"]'::jsonb,'["Liver biopsy to stage fibrosis before treatment", "Start prednisone to limit hepatic inflammation", "Start tenofovir and recheck liver enzymes in 1 week", "Supportive care and counseling on transmission", "Admit for IV N-acetylcysteine"]'::jsonb,'Supportive care and counseling on transmission','Supportive care and counseling on transmission','D',md5('A 19-year-old college student presents with jaundice, fatigue, and dark urine for 1 week. AST 1,240 U/L, ALT 1,580 U/L, total bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL. Anti-HAV IgM is positive.'),'A 19-year-old college student presents with jaundice, fatigue, and dark urine for 1 week. AST 1,240 U/L, ALT 1,580 U/L, total bilirubin 6.2 mg/dL. Anti-HAV IgM is positive.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Acute hepatitis A is self-limited; management is supportive with hygiene counseling. Tenofovir treats HBV, not HAV. Biopsy is not indicated for typical acute viral hepatitis. Ribavirin prophylaxis is not standard for HAV contacts.'),'Acute hepatitis A is self-limited, so management is supportive care plus counseling on transmission and contacts'' post-exposure prophylaxis (CDC 2020 ACIP HAV; AASLD). Why not: NAC is for acetaminophen toxicity, and there is no acute liver failure (no INR rise or encephalopathy). Tenofovir is for HBV. Steroids and biopsy are not indicated.'),
('cmqgdhxzh000o1yr8d8gfte03','["RIPE therapy (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol)", "Azithromycin monotherapy", "Fluconazole for 2 weeks", "Isoniazid monotherapy for 9 months"]'::jsonb,'["Start ART first and defer TB therapy for 8 weeks", "Azithromycin and ethambutol for presumed MAC infection", "Rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol", "Isoniazid and rifapentine weekly for 12 weeks", "Rifampin and isoniazid daily for 4 months"]'::jsonb,'RIPE therapy (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol)','Rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol','C',md5('A 40-year-old man with HIV (CD4 180 cells/µL, not on ART) presents with 2 weeks of cough, night sweats, and 5-kg weight loss. Temp 38.2°C. Chest X-ray shows upper lobe cavitary infiltrate. Sputum AFB smear is positive.'),'A 40-year-old man with HIV (CD4 180 cells/µL, not on ART) presents with 2 weeks of cough, night sweats, and 5-kg weight loss. Temp 38.2°C. Chest X-ray shows upper lobe cavitary infiltrate. Sputum AFB smear is positive.',md5('What is the most appropriate initial treatment?'),'What is the most appropriate initial treatment?',md5('Active pulmonary TB with cavitary disease and positive AFB requires standard four-drug RIPE induction. Azithromycin treats atypical pneumonia, not TB. Fluconazole treats fungal infection. Isoniazid alone is latent TB treatment, not active disease.'),'Active pulmonary TB in HIV is treated with the standard four-drug intensive phase (rifampin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) (ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016). ART starts within 2 weeks if CD4 is <50 and within 8 weeks otherwise (DHHS adult ART guideline), so ART is not given first while TB therapy waits. Rifampin plus isoniazid for 4 months is the continuation phase. Isoniazid-rifapentine weekly is latent TB treatment. Azithromycin-ethambutol treats MAC, which would not produce a positive AFB smear with a cavity this typical of TB.'),
('cmqgdi0jo001f1yr85orv24y5','["Emergent ophthalmology referral and topical fortified antibiotics", "Warm compresses and oral antihistamine", "Topical steroid drops alone", "Patch eye and follow up in 1 week"]'::jsonb,'["Lubricating drops and stop contact lens wear for 1 week", "Topical antiviral drops and recheck in 3 days", "Same-day ophthalmology referral and fortified antibiotics", "Topical steroid and antibiotic drops with recheck in 1 week", "Topical fluoroquinolone with outpatient follow-up in 1 week"]'::jsonb,'Emergent ophthalmology referral and topical fortified antibiotics','Same-day ophthalmology referral and fortified antibiotics','C',md5('A 60-year-old contact lens wearer presents with severe eye pain, photophobia, and decreased vision in one eye. BP 130/82 mm Hg, HR 88/min. Exam shows a corneal infiltrate with hypopyon.'),'A 60-year-old contact lens wearer presents with severe eye pain, photophobia, and decreased vision in one eye. BP 130/82 mm Hg, HR 88/min. Exam shows a corneal infiltrate with hypopyon.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Contact lens keratitis with infiltrate and hypopyon is a vision-threatening corneal ulcer — urgent ophthalmology and fortified topical antibiotics. Steroids alone risk perforation. Delayed follow-up is unsafe.'),'A contact lens wearer with a corneal infiltrate and hypopyon has bacterial keratitis (often Pseudomonas). This needs same-day ophthalmology care and intensive fortified antibiotics (AAO Bacterial Keratitis PPP 2023). Antiviral drops treat HSV dendrites. Steroids before organism control worsen the infection. A standard fluoroquinolone with 1-week follow-up undertreats a central ulcer with hypopyon. Lubrication and lens holiday are for minor lens-related irritation.'),
('cmqghstgy000q1y91032ht4mq','["Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state", "Gastroenteritis", "Appendicitis"]'::jsonb,'["Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state", "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Gastroenteritis", "Acute gastroparesis", "Appendicitis"]'::jsonb,'Diabetic ketoacidosis','Diabetic ketoacidosis','B',md5('A 10-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with a 2-hour history of nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. She has a history of type 1 diabetes and missed her insulin dose this morning. On examination, she is tachycardic with a heart rate of 120 bpm and has a blood glucose level of 400 mg/dL.'),'A 10-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with a 2-hour history of nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain. She has a history of type 1 diabetes and missed her insulin dose this morning. On examination, she is tachycardic with a heart rate of 120 bpm and has a blood glucose level of 400 mg/dL. Urine ketones are large and serum bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms of nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and significantly elevated blood glucose level, along with her history of type 1 diabetes, strongly suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). DKA is characterized by hyperglycemia, ketonemia, and metabolic acidosis.

Clinical reasoning: Given the patient''s missed insulin dose and the acute presentation of symptoms, DKA is the most likely diagnosis as it commonly occurs in patients with type 1 diabetes who have inadequate insulin.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state: While this condition also presents with hyperglycemia, it typically occurs in older adults with type 2 diabetes and is characterized by a higher level of dehydration and absence of significant ketones.
• Gastroenteritis: Gastroenteritis would usually present with diarrhea and is less likely to cause such a significant elevation in blood glucose levels.
• Appendicitis: Appendicitis would present with localized abdominal pain, fever, and possibly rebound tenderness, which are not indicated in this vignette.'),'A child with type 1 diabetes who missed insulin and has vomiting, tachycardia, glucose 400 mg/dL, large ketonuria and a bicarbonate of 12 mEq/L has DKA (ISPAD 2022 DKA guideline: glucose >200, pH <7.3 or HCO3 <18, ketosis). HHS has glucose >600 mg/dL with minimal ketosis. Gastroenteritis and appendicitis do not explain the ketoacidosis. Gastroparesis does not cause acidosis.'),
('cmqgqkq04001c1ydwo1tpww8r','["Start insulin therapy", "Initiate an ACE inhibitor", "Refer to a nephrologist", "Order a 24-hour urine collection"]'::jsonb,'["Restrict dietary protein to 0.4 g/kg/day", "Refer to a nephrologist", "Initiate an ACE inhibitor", "Start a thiazide diuretic", "Start amlodipine for blood pressure control"]'::jsonb,'Initiate an ACE inhibitor','Initiate an ACE inhibitor','C',md5('A 50-year-old woman presents to the clinic with complaints of frequent urination and nocturia. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Laboratory tests reveal a creatinine level of 1.2 mg/dL and a urinalysis shows proteinuria.'),'A 50-year-old woman presents to the clinic with complaints of frequent urination and nocturia. She has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. BP is 146/90 mm Hg on two visits, HbA1c is 7.8%, creatinine is 1.2 mg/dL (eGFR 58 mL/min/1.73 m²), and the spot urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 420 mg/g on two samples 3 months apart. She takes metformin only.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Initiating an ACE inhibitor is appropriate for managing hypertension and reducing proteinuria in patients with diabetes, which can help protect renal function.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s proteinuria and diabetes indicate a need for renal protective therapy, and ACE inhibitors are first-line agents for this purpose.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Start insulin therapy: Insulin therapy is not indicated based on the current symptoms and lab findings; the focus should be on managing hypertension and proteinuria.
• Refer to a nephrologist: Referral may be necessary later, but the immediate step is to start treatment for hypertension and proteinuria.
• Order a 24-hour urine collection: While a 24-hour urine collection can provide additional information, it is not necessary before initiating treatment with an ACE inhibitor.'),'Type 2 diabetes with hypertension and confirmed albuminuria (UACR 420 mg/g on two samples) calls for an ACE inhibitor or ARB titrated to the maximum tolerated dose (ADA Standards of Care 2026; KDIGO 2024 CKD). An SGLT2 inhibitor is also recommended at eGFR >=20 as an add-on. Amlodipine and thiazides lower BP but do not reduce albuminuria as RAS blockade does. Nephrology referral is advised for eGFR <30 or rapid decline, and her eGFR is 58. Restricting protein to 0.4 g/kg/day is not recommended; the target is 0.8 g/kg/day.'),
('cmqgmv8oi001v1yc5wlyf95b0','["Conduct a psychiatric evaluation", "Increase the dose of sertraline", "Discharge with outpatient follow-up", "Initiate electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)"]'::jsonb,'["Conduct a psychiatric evaluation", "Initiate electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)", "Discharge with outpatient follow-up", "Start a short benzodiazepine course", "Increase the dose of sertraline"]'::jsonb,'Conduct a psychiatric evaluation','Conduct a psychiatric evaluation','A',md5('A 35-year-old woman with a history of depression presents to the emergency department after a suicide attempt. She is currently taking sertraline and reports feeling hopeless. The medical team is concerned about her safety.'),'A 35-year-old woman with a history of depression presents to the emergency department after a suicide attempt by ingesting 30 sertraline tablets 8 hours ago. Vital signs, ECG and labs are normal, and toxicology has cleared her as medically stable. She is currently taking sertraline and reports feeling hopeless. The medical team is concerned about her safety.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('A psychiatric evaluation is crucial to assess the patient''s risk of self-harm and to determine the appropriate level of care and intervention.

Clinical reasoning: Given the patient''s recent suicide attempt and ongoing feelings of hopelessness, immediate psychiatric assessment is essential for her safety.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Increase the dose of sertraline: This is not appropriate in the acute setting, especially after a suicide attempt.
• Discharge with outpatient follow-up: This would not be safe given the patient''s current risk status.
• Initiate electroconvulsive therapy (ECT): ECT is not a first-line treatment in this acute context and requires careful consideration and evaluation.'),'Once she is medically cleared after an attempt, the next step is a full psychiatric evaluation with suicide-risk assessment to decide the level of care (APA practice guideline; Joint Commission NPSG 15.01.01). Why not: discharge is unsafe before assessment. ECT is not first-line here. Benzodiazepines don''t treat the risk. Raising the sertraline dose right after an overdose is inappropriate.'),
('cmqgdhwrl000b1yr8w1m5y7xj','["Short-acting bronchodilator, systemic corticosteroid, and antibiotic", "Supplemental oxygen to maintain SpO\u2082 100%", "Immediate intubation", "Antibiotic monotherapy without bronchodilators"]'::jsonb,'["Short-acting bronchodilator, systemic corticosteroid, and antibiotic", "Noninvasive ventilation and IV aminophylline infusion", "Short-acting bronchodilator and systemic corticosteroid, no antibiotic", "Add inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting bronchodilator, then reassess", "Short-acting bronchodilator, IV magnesium, and antibiotic"]'::jsonb,'Short-acting bronchodilator, systemic corticosteroid, and antibiotic','Short-acting bronchodilator, systemic corticosteroid, and antibiotic','A',md5('A 68-year-old man with COPD (FEV₁ 45% predicted) presents with increased dyspnea and purulent sputum for 3 days. Temp 38.2°C, RR 28/min, BP 130/82 mm Hg, SpO₂ 88% on room air. WBC 14.2 × 10³/µL. Chest X-ray shows no infiltrate.'),'A 68-year-old man with COPD (FEV₁ 45% predicted) presents with increased dyspnea and purulent sputum for 3 days. Temp 38.2°C, RR 28/min, BP 130/82 mm Hg, SpO₂ 88% on room air, now 90% on 2 L nasal cannula. WBC 14.2 × 10³/µL. Chest X-ray shows no infiltrate.',md5('Which is the most appropriate initial management?'),'Which is the most appropriate initial management?',md5('Acute COPD exacerbation with increased sputum purulence warrants bronchodilators, steroids, and antibiotics per GOLD. Target SpO₂ 88–92% in COPD to avoid suppressing hypoxic drive. NIPPV is considered for persistent hypercapnia/acidosis, not routine intubation. Antibiotics alone miss bronchodilator and anti-inflammatory therapy.'),'GOLD 2026: for an exacerbation with increased dyspnea and purulent sputum, give a SABA ± SAMA, systemic corticosteroid (prednisone 40 mg for 5 days) and an antibiotic, with O2 titrated to 88-92%. Why not: IV magnesium lacks COPD evidence. Aminophylline is not recommended. Omitting the antibiotic ignores the purulent sputum. ICS/LABA is maintenance therapy.'),
('cmqgdhx8k000g1yr8tsp8guvq','["Upper endoscopy", "Trial of PPI for 8 weeks before any testing", "Barium swallow only", "Outpatient H. pylori serology"]'::jsonb,'["Esophageal manometry", "Trial of PPI for 8 weeks before testing", "Barium esophagram", "Upper endoscopy", "H. pylori stool antigen testing"]'::jsonb,'Upper endoscopy','Upper endoscopy','D',md5('A 45-year-old woman with long-standing GERD reports dysphagia to solids for 3 months and 4-kg weight loss. She denies odynophagia. Hgb 11.2 g/dL. She has not had endoscopy in 10 years.'),'A 45-year-old woman with long-standing GERD reports dysphagia to solids for 3 months and 4-kg weight loss. She denies odynophagia. Hgb 11.2 g/dL. She has not had endoscopy in 10 years.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step?'),'What is the most appropriate next step?',md5('Progressive dysphagia to solids and weight loss in chronic GERD warrants endoscopy to exclude malignancy and assess for stricture/Barrett esophagus. Empiric PPI alone delays diagnosis. Barium swallow is inferior for mucosal disease and biopsy.'),'Progressive dysphagia to solids and weight loss in chronic GERD warrants endoscopy to exclude malignancy and assess for stricture/Barrett esophagus. Empiric PPI alone delays diagnosis. Barium swallow is inferior for mucosal disease and biopsy.

Why not "Trial of PPI for 8 weeks before testing": dysphagia, weight loss and anemia are alarm features that require endoscopy first (ACG 2022).
Why not "Barium esophagram": it cannot biopsy and does not replace EGD for alarm features.
Why not "H. pylori stool antigen testing": it does not evaluate dysphagia.
Why not "Esophageal manometry": manometry follows a normal EGD.'),
('cmqgdhxpf000l1yr8wmzd23e3','["Urgent MRI lumbar spine", "Bed rest for 4 weeks", "Oral opioid monotherapy", "Immediate surgical fusion"]'::jsonb,'["Electromyography in 3 weeks", "Lumbar epidural steroid injection", "NSAIDs and plain lumbar radiographs", "Physical therapy and reassess in 6 weeks", "Urgent MRI lumbar spine"]'::jsonb,'Urgent MRI lumbar spine','Urgent MRI lumbar spine','E',md5('A 58-year-old warehouse worker presents with low back pain radiating to the left leg for 2 days after lifting a box. BP 132/84 mm Hg, HR 88/min. Straight leg raise is positive at 40° on the left. He has foot drop and decreased sensation over the lateral foot. Bladder function is normal.'),'A 58-year-old warehouse worker presents with low back pain radiating to the left leg for 2 days after lifting a box. BP 132/84 mm Hg, HR 88/min. Straight leg raise is positive at 40° on the left. He has foot drop and decreased sensation over the lateral foot. Bladder function is normal.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step?'),'What is the most appropriate next step?',md5('Progressive neurologic deficit (foot drop) with radicular pain warrants urgent imaging to evaluate cord/cauda equina compression. Bed rest alone risks permanent deficit. Surgery is not first-line without imaging and unless cauda equina is present.'),'Progressive neurologic deficit (foot drop) with radicular pain warrants urgent imaging to evaluate cord/cauda equina compression. Bed rest alone risks permanent deficit. Surgery is not first-line without imaging and unless cauda equina is present.

Why not "Physical therapy and reassess in 6 weeks": foot drop is a progressive motor deficit, which is a red flag needing urgent MRI (ACR Appropriateness Criteria low back pain 2021).
Why not "NSAIDs and plain lumbar radiographs": radiographs do not show disc or nerve compression.
Why not "Electromyography in 3 weeks": EMG does not replace imaging when there is a motor deficit.
Why not "Lumbar epidural steroid injection": injection without imaging is not appropriate with foot drop.'),
('cmqgdhy9i000r1yr83kaqdiry','["IV antibiotics pending culture and orthopedic drainage if no improvement", "Oral colchicine", "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "NSAIDs alone and follow-up in 2 weeks"]'::jsonb,'["IV antibiotics and prompt joint drainage", "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "Oral antibiotics and recheck in 1 week", "IV antibiotics and drainage if no response in 72 hours", "Oral colchicine and NSAIDs"]'::jsonb,'IV antibiotics pending culture and orthopedic drainage if no improvement','IV antibiotics and prompt joint drainage','A',md5('A 65-year-old woman presents with acute monoarticular knee pain and swelling. Temp 38.2°C. Synovial fluid shows WBC 85,000/µL with 90% neutrophils, no crystals, Gram stain negative.'),'A 65-year-old woman presents with acute monoarticular knee pain and swelling. Temp 38.2°C. Synovial fluid shows WBC 85,000/µL with 90% neutrophils, no crystals, Gram stain negative.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Highly inflammatory synovial fluid in a febrile patient is septic arthritis until proven otherwise — empiric IV antibiotics and possible joint drainage. Colchicine treats gout (would show crystals). Steroids without ruling out infection worsen outcomes.'),'Synovial WBC 85,000/uL with neutrophil predominance and no crystals is septic arthritis until proven otherwise, even with a negative Gram stain. It needs empiric IV antibiotics plus prompt joint drainage (needle aspiration, arthroscopic or open) (BSR/BHPR hot swollen joint guideline 2006; StatPearls Septic Arthritis 2024). Delaying drainage until there is no response risks cartilage destruction. Colchicine and intra-articular steroids treat crystal arthritis and are harmful in infection. Oral antibiotics with a 1-week recheck undertreat it.'),
('cmqgdhyqq000w1yr8m5d2wt4g','["Mood stabilizer such as valproate or atypical antipsychotic", "SSRI monotherapy", "Cholinesterase inhibitor", "Stimulant augmentation"]'::jsonb,'["Stimulant for attention and focus", "Bupropion with sleep hygiene counseling", "Atypical antipsychotic such as aripiprazole or quetiapine", "SSRI with a short benzodiazepine course", "Lamotrigine titrated slowly over 6 weeks as the first agent"]'::jsonb,'Mood stabilizer such as valproate or atypical antipsychotic','Atypical antipsychotic such as aripiprazole or quetiapine','C',md5('A 19-year-old college student presents with 5 days of decreased sleep, pressured speech, grandiose ideas, and risky spending. BP 132/84 mm Hg, HR 96/min. No substance use. She is alert and oriented.'),'A 19-year-old college student presents with 8 days of sleeping 2 hours a night, pressured speech, grandiose ideas, and risky spending that have stopped her from attending classes. BP 132/84 mm Hg, HR 96/min. No substance use. She is alert and oriented.',md5('What is the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy?'),'What is the most appropriate initial pharmacotherapy?',md5('Acute mania requires mood stabilizer or atypical antipsychotic — SSRIs alone can worsen mania. Stimulants are contraindicated. Cholinesterase inhibitors treat dementia.'),'Eight days of decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, grandiosity and risky spending, with loss of function, meet DSM-5-TR criteria for a manic episode (>=7 days). First-line acute treatment is lithium, valproate or an atypical antipsychotic such as quetiapine, aripiprazole, risperidone or asenapine (CANMAT/ISBD 2018). In a young woman, valproate is avoided because of its teratogenic risk (MHRA/EMA 2018), so an atypical antipsychotic is preferred. An SSRI can worsen mania. Lamotrigine is for maintenance and bipolar depression and takes weeks to titrate. Stimulants and bupropion worsen mania.'),
('cmqgdhyu6000x1yr8ihh5gojy','["Benzodiazepines (e.g. lorazepam) with thiamine before glucose", "Disulfiram loading dose", "Haloperidol monotherapy without benzodiazepine", "Observation only"]'::jsonb,'["Naltrexone in the ED and outpatient follow-up", "Gabapentin and clonidine for autonomic symptoms", "Lorazepam and IV thiamine", "IV dextrose, then thiamine", "Haloperidol for hallucinations"]'::jsonb,'Benzodiazepines (e.g. lorazepam) with thiamine before glucose','Lorazepam and IV thiamine','C',md5('A 55-year-old man with alcohol use disorder presents with tremor, diaphoresis, and visual hallucinations 18 hours after his last drink. BP 162/98 mm Hg, HR 112/min.'),'A 55-year-old man with alcohol use disorder presents with tremor, diaphoresis, and visual hallucinations 18 hours after his last drink. BP 162/98 mm Hg, HR 112/min.',md5('What is the most appropriate initial management?'),'What is the most appropriate initial management?',md5('Alcohol withdrawal with autonomic hyperactivity and hallucinations requires benzodiazepines to prevent seizures/delirium tremens; thiamine before glucose prevents Wernicke. Haloperidol alone does not prevent seizures. Disulfiram is for abstinence maintenance, not acute withdrawal.'),'Alcohol withdrawal with autonomic hyperactivity and hallucinations: benzodiazepines are first-line, and thiamine should be given before glucose (ASAM 2020 alcohol withdrawal guideline). Why not: haloperidol lowers the seizure threshold and is not monotherapy. Gabapentin and clonidine are adjuncts or for mild withdrawal. Naltrexone is for relapse prevention. Dextrose before thiamine risks Wernicke''s.'),
('cmqgdhyxk000y1yr82zfew59w','["Postpartum obsessive-compulsive disorder", "Postpartum psychosis", "Normal new-parent anxiety requiring no intervention", "Munchausen syndrome by proxy"]'::jsonb,'["Postpartum depression with anxious distress", "Postpartum blues", "Generalized anxiety disorder", "Postpartum obsessive-compulsive disorder", "Postpartum psychosis"]'::jsonb,'Postpartum obsessive-compulsive disorder','Postpartum obsessive-compulsive disorder','D',md5('A 28-year-old woman reports intrusive thoughts of harming her newborn and avoids being alone with the baby. She has no plan to act and is distressed by the thoughts. BP 118/72 mm Hg, HR 80/min. Mood is euthymic.'),'A 28-year-old woman 3 weeks after delivery reports intrusive thoughts of harming her newborn and avoids being alone with the baby. She has no plan to act and is distressed by the thoughts. BP 118/72 mm Hg, HR 80/min. Mood is euthymic.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('Ego-dystonic intrusive harm thoughts without intent or psychosis suggests postpartum OCD — treat with SSRI/CBT. Postpartum psychosis includes delusions, disorganization, and high suicide/infanticide risk. Normal anxiety lacks intrusive obsessions.'),'Ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts of harming the infant 3 weeks after delivery, with avoidance, intact insight, no plan and euthymic mood, are postpartum OCD (ACOG Clinical Practice Guideline 5, 2023). Postpartum psychosis has delusions, confusion and impaired insight. Postpartum depression needs depressed mood or anhedonia. Postpartum blues resolve by 2 weeks and do not cause intrusive harm thoughts. GAD worry is diffuse, not intrusive harm images.'),
('cmqgqkqwq001n1ydwck3mt81g','["Refer for coagulation studies", "Advise humidification and nasal saline", "Prescribe topical vasoconstrictors", "Perform cauterization of the nasal mucosa"]'::jsonb,'["Topical oxymetazoline daily for 2 weeks", "Advise humidification and nasal saline", "CT of the sinuses and nasal cavity", "Refer for coagulation studies", "Silver nitrate cautery of the septum"]'::jsonb,'Advise humidification and nasal saline','Advise humidification and nasal saline','B',md5('A 30-year-old male presents to the surgical clinic for evaluation of recurrent nosebleeds. He reports episodes occurring several times a month, often triggered by dry air or minor trauma. He has no significant past medical history and takes no medications.'),'A 30-year-old male presents to the primary care clinic for evaluation of recurrent nosebleeds. He reports episodes occurring several times a month, often triggered by dry air or minor trauma. He has no significant past medical history and takes no medications. The anterior septal mucosa is dry and crusted, with no active bleeding or visible vessel, and there is no family history of bleeding.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('For recurrent nosebleeds, especially in a patient with no significant medical history, the first step is to address environmental factors. Humidification and nasal saline can help keep the nasal mucosa moist and reduce the frequency of bleeds.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette indicates that the nosebleeds are likely related to environmental dryness, making humidification and saline the most appropriate initial management.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Refer for coagulation studies: This is unnecessary at this stage since the patient has no significant medical history or signs of a bleeding disorder.
• Prescribe topical vasoconstrictors: While these can be used, they are not the first-line treatment for recurrent nosebleeds without other underlying issues.
• Perform cauterization of the nasal mucosa: Cauterization is typically reserved for persistent cases and is not the first step in management.'),'Recurrent anterior epistaxis from dry mucosa with no visible vessel: moisturize with humidification and nasal saline or petroleum jelly (AAO-HNS 2020 epistaxis CPG). Why not: cautery needs an identifiable bleeding site. Daily oxymetazoline causes rebound. CT and coagulation studies are not indicated without red flags or a family history.'),
('cmqgrj6o2000q1yxdeva2kibc','["Initiate hemodialysis", "Discontinue ibuprofen", "Start intravenous fluids", "Order a renal ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'["Discontinue ibuprofen", "Start oral prednisone", "Order a renal ultrasound", "Initiate hemodialysis", "Start intravenous fluids"]'::jsonb,'Discontinue ibuprofen','Discontinue ibuprofen','A',md5('A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute renal failure. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. Laboratory tests reveal a creatinine level of 3.5 mg/dL and a BUN of 40 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows no proteinuria. She has been taking ibuprofen for chronic pain management.'),'A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute renal failure. She has a history of diabetes and hypertension. Laboratory tests reveal a creatinine level of 3.5 mg/dL and a BUN of 40 mg/dL. Urinalysis shows no proteinuria. She is euvolemic on exam, and a bladder scan shows no retention. She has been taking ibuprofen for chronic pain management.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('In this case, the patient''s acute renal failure is likely exacerbated by the use of ibuprofen, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that can impair renal function, especially in patients with pre-existing conditions like diabetes and hypertension. The most appropriate next step is to discontinue ibuprofen to prevent further renal injury. Initiating hemodialysis may be necessary later if renal function does not improve, but it is not the immediate step. Starting intravenous fluids can help, but addressing the causative factor is paramount. A renal ultrasound may be useful later for evaluation but is not the immediate priority.'),'NSAID-associated AKI in a euvolemic patient with no obstruction is managed first by stopping the NSAID (KDIGO AKI 2012: stop nephrotoxins). IV fluids are for hypovolemia, which the exam excludes. A bladder scan showing no retention makes obstruction unlikely, so ultrasound is not the first step. Hemodialysis needs an urgent indication (refractory K, acidosis, overload, uremia). Prednisone is only for biopsy-proven interstitial nephritis.'),
('cmqgqv00s000d1y4v7pwbsto0','["Hospitalize the patient for safety", "Initiate a new antidepressant", "Refer to outpatient therapy", "Contact family for support"]'::jsonb,'["Refer to outpatient therapy", "Hospitalize the patient for safety", "Contact family for support", "Discharge with a safety plan and outpatient follow-up", "Initiate a new antidepressant"]'::jsonb,'Hospitalize the patient for safety','Hospitalize the patient for safety','B',md5('A 25-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with suicidal ideation and a plan to harm herself. She has a history of depression and has recently stopped taking her antidepressant medication. On examination, she appears withdrawn and tearful. Her vital signs are stable.'),'A 25-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with suicidal ideation and a plan to harm herself. She has a history of depression and has recently stopped taking her antidepressant medication. On examination, she appears withdrawn and tearful. Her vital signs are stable.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Given the patient''s suicidal ideation and plan, the immediate priority is to ensure her safety through hospitalization. This allows for close monitoring and intervention as needed.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette describes a patient at high risk for self-harm, necessitating hospitalization to provide a safe environment and appropriate psychiatric care.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Initiate a new antidepressant: While medication may be necessary, the immediate concern is the patient''s safety, not medication management.
• Refer to outpatient therapy: This is not appropriate given the acute risk of self-harm; inpatient care is required.
• Contact family for support: While family support is important, it does not address the immediate risk of suicide.'),'Given the patient''s suicidal ideation and plan, the immediate priority is to ensure her safety through hospitalization. This allows for close monitoring and intervention as needed.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette describes a patient at high risk for self-harm, necessitating hospitalization to provide a safe environment and appropriate psychiatric care.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Initiate a new antidepressant: While medication may be necessary, the immediate concern is the patient''s safety, not medication management.
• Refer to outpatient therapy: This is not appropriate given the acute risk of self-harm; inpatient care is required.
• Contact family for support: While family support is important, it does not address the immediate risk of suicide.

Why not "Discharge with a safety plan and outpatient follow-up": a plan with intent and recent discontinuation of treatment is high acute risk, which needs inpatient care rather than outpatient safety planning.'),
('cmqgrcc8900021yxd83ioanv1','["Septic shock", "Acute kidney injury", "Pneumonia", "Urinary tract infection"]'::jsonb,'["Acute kidney injury", "Septic shock", "Pneumonia", "Urinary tract infection", "Hypovolemic shock"]'::jsonb,'Septic shock','Septic shock','B',md5('A 65-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with fever, chills, and confusion. She has a history of diabetes and chronic kidney disease. Vital signs show a temperature of 39.4°C, heart rate of 110 bpm, and blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. Laboratory tests reveal elevated creatinine and a lactate level of 4.5 mmol/L.'),'A 65-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with fever, chills, and confusion. She has a history of diabetes and chronic kidney disease. Vital signs show a temperature of 39.4°C, heart rate of 110 bpm, and blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. Laboratory tests reveal elevated creatinine and a lactate level of 4.5 mmol/L.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s presentation of fever, confusion, and hypotension, along with elevated lactate levels, strongly suggests septic shock, which is a severe manifestation of sepsis characterized by persistent hypotension despite adequate fluid resuscitation. The history of diabetes and chronic kidney disease increases her risk for infections leading to sepsis. Acute kidney injury is a potential complication but does not explain the systemic signs of infection and shock. Pneumonia and urinary tract infection could be sources of sepsis but are not definitive diagnoses without further investigation.'),'The patient''s presentation of fever, confusion, and hypotension, along with elevated lactate levels, strongly suggests septic shock, which is a severe manifestation of sepsis characterized by persistent hypotension despite adequate fluid resuscitation. The history of diabetes and chronic kidney disease increases her risk for infections leading to sepsis. Acute kidney injury is a potential complication but does not explain the systemic signs of infection and shock. Pneumonia and urinary tract infection could be sources of sepsis but are not definitive diagnoses without further investigation.

Why not "Hypovolemic shock": Fever, confusion and lactate 4.5 with hypotension define septic shock (Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021).'),
('cmqgsq08l000a1yy7i6mhp407','["Acute glomerulonephritis", "Acute tubular necrosis", "Prerenal acute kidney injury", "Postrenal acute kidney injury"]'::jsonb,'["Acute glomerulonephritis", "Acute tubular necrosis", "Postrenal acute kidney injury", "Prerenal acute kidney injury", "Rhabdomyolysis-induced AKI"]'::jsonb,'Prerenal acute kidney injury','Prerenal acute kidney injury','D',md5('A 5-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of decreased urine output and lethargy. His mother reports he had a recent upper respiratory infection and has been drinking less fluid. On examination, he is tachycardic with a heart rate of 120/min, and his blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. Laboratory tests reveal elevated creatinine levels and a BUN/creatinine ratio of 20:1.'),'A 5-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of decreased urine output and lethargy. His mother reports he had a recent upper respiratory infection and has been drinking less fluid. On examination, he is tachycardic with a heart rate of 120/min, and his blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. Laboratory tests reveal elevated creatinine levels and a BUN/creatinine ratio of 20:1.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('This child''s presentation is consistent with prerenal acute kidney injury (AKI) due to dehydration following a recent upper respiratory infection. The elevated BUN/creatinine ratio suggests a prerenal cause, as it typically exceeds 20:1 in such cases. Acute glomerulonephritis would present with hematuria and proteinuria, which are not mentioned here. Acute tubular necrosis usually follows ischemia or nephrotoxins and would not typically present with a history of dehydration alone. Postrenal AKI would require evidence of obstruction, which is not indicated in this vignette.'),'This child''s presentation is consistent with prerenal acute kidney injury (AKI) due to dehydration following a recent upper respiratory infection. The elevated BUN/creatinine ratio suggests a prerenal cause, as it typically exceeds 20:1 in such cases. Acute glomerulonephritis would present with hematuria and proteinuria, which are not mentioned here. Acute tubular necrosis usually follows ischemia or nephrotoxins and would not typically present with a history of dehydration alone. Postrenal AKI would require evidence of obstruction, which is not indicated in this vignette.

Why not "Rhabdomyolysis-induced AKI": There is no muscle injury; dehydration with BUN/Cr 20:1 is prerenal.'),
('cmqgz640d001a1yqyc8kosyxy','["Surgical repair", "Watchful waiting", "Referral for imaging", "Compression garment"]'::jsonb,'["Emergency laparotomy", "Hernia truss", "Surgical repair", "Groin ultrasound before referral", "Watchful waiting"]'::jsonb,'Surgical repair','Surgical repair','C',md5('A 60-year-old man presents to the surgical clinic for evaluation of a suspected inguinal hernia. He reports a bulge in the right groin that becomes more prominent with straining and reduces when lying down. He has a history of obesity and chronic cough. On examination, there is a palpable mass in the right inguinal region.'),'A 60-year-old man presents to the surgical clinic for evaluation of a suspected inguinal hernia. He reports a bulge in the right groin that becomes more prominent with straining and reduces when lying down. The bulge causes groin pain that now limits his work. He has a history of obesity and chronic cough. On examination, there is a palpable mass in the right inguinal region.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('The correct answer is surgical repair, as this patient has a symptomatic inguinal hernia that is reducible. Surgical intervention is the definitive treatment to prevent complications such as incarceration or strangulation. Option watchful waiting is incorrect because it is typically reserved for asymptomatic hernias. Option referral for imaging is incorrect as imaging is not necessary in this case where the diagnosis is clinically evident. Option compression garment is incorrect because while it may provide temporary relief, it does not address the underlying hernia.'),'A reducible inguinal hernia that causes pain and limits activity should be repaired electively (HerniaSurge International Guidelines 2018). Watchful waiting is reasonable only for minimally symptomatic men. Ultrasound is unnecessary when the hernia is clinically obvious. Trusses do not prevent progression and are not recommended. Emergency surgery is for incarceration or strangulation.'),
('cmqgzabyc006w1yqytdql8i26','["Acute pancreatitis", "Peptic ulcer disease", "Cholecystitis", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease"]'::jsonb,'["Peptic ulcer disease", "Cholecystitis", "Acute hepatitis", "Acute pancreatitis", "Gastroesophageal reflux disease"]'::jsonb,'Acute pancreatitis','Acute pancreatitis','D',md5('A 60-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back. He has a history of chronic alcohol use and is currently taking metformin for type 2 diabetes. His vital signs show a BP of 90/60 mm Hg, HR of 110/min, and a serum amylase level of 300 U/L. On examination, he has tenderness in the epigastric region.'),'A 60-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back. He has a history of chronic alcohol use and is currently taking metformin for type 2 diabetes. His vital signs show a BP of 90/60 mm Hg, HR of 110/min, and a serum amylase level of 300 U/L. On examination, he has tenderness in the epigastric region.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The correct answer is acute pancreatitis, which is characterized by severe epigastric pain, tenderness, and elevated serum amylase levels. The patient''s history of chronic alcohol use is a significant risk factor for developing acute pancreatitis. The pain radiating to the back and the vital signs indicating possible hypovolemia further support this diagnosis. Option peptic ulcer disease is incorrect because it typically presents with gnawing pain and is less likely to cause elevated amylase levels. Option cholecystitis is incorrect as it usually presents with right upper quadrant pain and may have associated fever and Murphy''s sign. Option gastroesophageal reflux disease is incorrect because it typically presents with heartburn and regurgitation rather than severe epigastric pain.'),'The correct answer is acute pancreatitis, which is characterized by severe epigastric pain, tenderness, and elevated serum amylase levels. The patient''s history of chronic alcohol use is a significant risk factor for developing acute pancreatitis. The pain radiating to the back and the vital signs indicating possible hypovolemia further support this diagnosis. Option peptic ulcer disease is incorrect because it typically presents with gnawing pain and is less likely to cause elevated amylase levels. Option cholecystitis is incorrect as it usually presents with right upper quadrant pain and may have associated fever and Murphy''s sign. Option gastroesophageal reflux disease is incorrect because it typically presents with heartburn and regurgitation rather than severe epigastric pain.

Why not "Acute hepatitis": Epigastric pain radiating to the back with raised amylase fits pancreatitis, not hepatitis.'),
('cmqgzdqh4000k1ydkws60piut','["Cartilage degeneration", "Synovial inflammation", "Autoimmune response", "Infection"]'::jsonb,'["Crystal deposition", "Synovial inflammation", "Autoimmune response", "Cartilage degeneration", "Bacterial joint infection"]'::jsonb,'Cartilage degeneration','Cartilage degeneration','D',md5('A 65-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with complaints of joint stiffness and pain in her hands and knees. She has a family history of osteoarthritis and reports that her symptoms worsen with activity. Physical examination reveals crepitus and tenderness in the affected joints.'),'A 65-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with complaints of joint stiffness and pain in her hands and knees. Her symptoms worsen with activity, and morning stiffness lasts less than 30 minutes. Physical examination reveals crepitus and tenderness in the affected joints.',md5('What is the best explanation for the findings?'),'What is the best explanation for the findings?',md5('The correct answer is cartilage degeneration, which is the primary pathological process in osteoarthritis, leading to joint pain and stiffness. This degeneration causes the characteristic symptoms and physical findings observed in this patient. Option Synovial inflammation is incorrect because it is more characteristic of rheumatoid arthritis rather than osteoarthritis, which is primarily due to mechanical wear and tear. Option Autoimmune response is incorrect as it does not explain the findings in osteoarthritis, which is not an autoimmune condition. Option Infection is incorrect because there are no signs of systemic infection or joint effusion that would suggest septic arthritis.'),'Activity-related pain in the hands and knees with crepitus and brief morning stiffness (<30 minutes) at age 65 is osteoarthritis, which is driven by cartilage degeneration (ACR/Arthritis Foundation OA guideline 2019). Synovial inflammation and autoimmunity are RA, which has prolonged morning stiffness and MCP synovitis. Infection is acute and monoarticular. Crystal deposition gives acute flares.'),
('cmqgzdoib000f1ydku70gs4mh','["Start a proton pump inhibitor", "Refer for surgical evaluation", "Order an upper endoscopy", "Increase antacid dosage"]'::jsonb,'["Start a proton pump inhibitor", "Start metoclopramide daily", "Order an upper endoscopy", "Start an H2 blocker as needed", "Refer for surgical evaluation"]'::jsonb,'Start a proton pump inhibitor','Start a proton pump inhibitor','A',md5('A 55-year-old woman presents to the surgical clinic with symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) for the past year. She reports heartburn after meals and regurgitation of sour fluid. She has tried over-the-counter antacids with minimal relief. Vital signs are stable, and her physical exam is unremarkable.'),'A 55-year-old woman presents to the primary care clinic with symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) for the past year. She reports heartburn after meals and regurgitation of sour fluid. She has tried over-the-counter antacids with minimal relief. Vital signs are stable, and her physical exam is unremarkable.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('The correct answer is to start a proton pump inhibitor (PPI), as this is the first-line treatment for GERD, especially in patients with persistent symptoms despite over-the-counter antacids. PPIs effectively reduce gastric acid secretion and provide symptomatic relief. 

Option Refer for surgical evaluation is incorrect because surgical intervention is typically reserved for patients with refractory symptoms or complications of GERD, which this patient does not have at this time. 

Option Order an upper endoscopy is incorrect as it is not necessary in the initial management of uncomplicated GERD unless alarm symptoms are present. 

Option Increase antacid dosage is incorrect because the patient has already reported minimal relief from antacids, indicating that a stronger intervention is required.'),'Typical GERD (heartburn and regurgitation) without alarm features is treated with an 8-week empiric PPI trial (ACG GERD guideline 2022). Endoscopy is for alarm features or PPI failure. Antireflux surgery is for refractory or PPI-dependent disease. An as-needed H2 blocker is less effective than a daily PPI for frequent symptoms. Metoclopramide is not recommended for GERD because of tardive dyskinesia risk.'),
('cmqwuof9p007h1yzedl5q8tbi','["Prescribe amoxicillin", "Perform a throat culture", "Administer corticosteroids", "Provide symptomatic treatment only"]'::jsonb,'["Administer corticosteroids", "Symptomatic care and recheck in 48 hours", "Prescribe amoxicillin", "Prescribe azithromycin", "Perform a throat culture"]'::jsonb,'Prescribe amoxicillin','Prescribe amoxicillin','C',md5('A 5-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of fever, sore throat, and difficulty swallowing. His temperature is 39.4°C, heart rate is 110/min, and he has swollen tonsils with exudate. A rapid strep test is positive. He has no known drug allergies.'),'A 5-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of fever, sore throat, and difficulty swallowing. His temperature is 39.4°C, heart rate is 110/min, and he has swollen tonsils with exudate. A rapid strep test is positive. He has no known drug allergies.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Amoxicillin is the first-line treatment for streptococcal pharyngitis in children, as it effectively eradicates the bacteria and reduces the risk of complications. Option Perform a throat culture is incorrect because the rapid strep test is already positive, indicating that immediate treatment is warranted. Option Administer corticosteroids is incorrect because corticosteroids do not address the bacterial infection and are not indicated in this case. Option Provide symptomatic treatment only is incorrect as it does not treat the underlying infection and can lead to complications such as rheumatic fever. Key takeaway: Prompt antibiotic treatment is essential in managing streptococcal pharyngitis to prevent complications.'),'A positive rapid strep test in a child with exudative pharyngitis is treated with amoxicillin or penicillin (IDSA 2012 GAS pharyngitis guideline). A positive RADT needs no confirmatory culture. Corticosteroids are not routine. Symptomatic care alone leaves the risk of rheumatic fever. Azithromycin is reserved for penicillin allergy and has higher resistance.'),
('cmr828byx004u1y8w5za40ha9','["Generalized Anxiety Disorder", "Panic Disorder", "Social Anxiety Disorder", "Adjustment Disorder with Anxiety"]'::jsonb,'["Persistent depressive disorder", "Adjustment Disorder with Anxiety", "Panic Disorder", "Social Anxiety Disorder", "Generalized Anxiety Disorder"]'::jsonb,'Generalized Anxiety Disorder','Generalized Anxiety Disorder','E',md5('A 30-year-old woman presents to the clinic with excessive worry and restlessness for the past 6 months. She reports difficulty sleeping and muscle tension, which has affected her work performance. Her BP is 130/85 mm Hg, HR is 78/min, and she has a GAD-7 score of 15. On examination, she appears anxious and fidgety.'),'A 30-year-old woman presents to the clinic with excessive worry and restlessness for the past 6 months. She reports difficulty sleeping and muscle tension, which has affected her work performance. Her BP is 130/85 mm Hg, HR is 78/min, and she has a GAD-7 score of 15. On examination, she appears anxious and fidgety.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The correct answer is Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) because the patient exhibits excessive anxiety and worry occurring more days than not for at least 6 months, along with physical symptoms such as restlessness and muscle tension. According to the DSM-5 criteria, GAD is characterized by persistent and excessive worry about various aspects of life. Option Panic Disorder is incorrect because it involves recurrent unexpected panic attacks and not generalized worry. Option Social Anxiety Disorder is incorrect as it specifically relates to fear of social situations rather than pervasive anxiety. Option Adjustment Disorder with Anxiety is incorrect because it is typically triggered by a specific stressor and does not last for more than 6 months. Key takeaway: GAD is characterized by chronic anxiety and worry across multiple domains of life.'),'The correct answer is Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) because the patient exhibits excessive anxiety and worry occurring more days than not for at least 6 months, along with physical symptoms such as restlessness and muscle tension. According to the DSM-5 criteria, GAD is characterized by persistent and excessive worry about various aspects of life. Option Panic Disorder is incorrect because it involves recurrent unexpected panic attacks and not generalized worry. Option Social Anxiety Disorder is incorrect as it specifically relates to fear of social situations rather than pervasive anxiety. Option Adjustment Disorder with Anxiety is incorrect because it is typically triggered by a specific stressor and does not last for more than 6 months. Key takeaway: GAD is characterized by chronic anxiety and worry across multiple domains of life.

Why not "Persistent depressive disorder": There is no chronic depressed mood; 6 months of excessive worry with tension fits GAD.'),
('cmqgz2vsi002k1ybcqxbjlx0p','["Acute coronary syndrome", "Pulmonary embolism", "Aortic dissection", "Pericarditis"]'::jsonb,'["Acute pericarditis", "Acute inferior STEMI", "Pulmonary embolism", "Aortic dissection", "Non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction"]'::jsonb,'Acute coronary syndrome','Acute inferior STEMI','B',md5('A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with chest pain and dyspnea. He has a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia. His vital signs show BP 150/90 mm Hg, HR 100/min, and an elevated troponin level of 1.5 ng/mL. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF.'),'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with chest pain and dyspnea. He has a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia. His vital signs show BP 150/90 mm Hg, HR 100/min, and an elevated troponin level of 1.5 ng/mL. An ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The correct answer is acute coronary syndrome (ACS), as the patient''s presentation of chest pain, dyspnea, elevated troponin levels, and ST-segment elevation in the inferior leads indicates myocardial ischemia, likely due to an occluded coronary artery. This is a classic presentation of an ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI). Option pulmonary embolism is incorrect because it would typically present with sudden onset dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain, not ST-segment elevation. Option aortic dissection is incorrect as it usually presents with severe, tearing chest pain and may show different ECG changes. Option pericarditis is incorrect because it typically presents with pleuritic chest pain that improves when leaning forward and would not show ST-segment elevation in the inferior leads in this manner.'),'Chest pain, troponin elevation and ST elevation in II, III and aVF are an acute inferior ST-elevation MI (Fourth Universal Definition of MI 2018; 2025 ACC/AHA ACS guideline). NSTEMI does not have ST elevation. Pericarditis gives diffuse ST elevation with PR depression. Aortic dissection and PE do not produce a localized inferior STEMI pattern with this troponin rise.'),
('cmqgz1a3r001h1ybc9tp8vtd6','["Start intravenous heparin", "Administer thrombolytics", "Initiate warfarin therapy", "Perform an inferior vena cava filter placement"]'::jsonb,'["Perform an inferior vena cava filter placement", "Start apixaban and discharge today", "Initiate warfarin therapy", "Administer thrombolytics", "Start intravenous heparin"]'::jsonb,'Start intravenous heparin','Start intravenous heparin','E',md5('A 30-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with sudden onset chest pain and shortness of breath after a long flight. She has a history of deep vein thrombosis and is currently on oral contraceptives. Vital signs show a blood pressure of 110/70 mm Hg, heart rate of 110 bpm, and oxygen saturation of 88% on room air. A CT pulmonary angiogram reveals a large pulmonary embolism.'),'A 30-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with sudden onset chest pain and shortness of breath after a long flight. She has a history of deep vein thrombosis and is currently on oral contraceptives. Vital signs show a blood pressure of 110/70 mm Hg, heart rate of 110 bpm, and oxygen saturation of 88% on room air. A CT pulmonary angiogram reveals a large pulmonary embolism. Echocardiogram shows normal right ventricular size and function, and troponin is normal.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('The correct answer is to start intravenous heparin, as it is the immediate treatment for a confirmed pulmonary embolism to prevent further clot propagation. This is a critical step in managing the patient''s acute condition. Option administer thrombolytics is incorrect because while they may be indicated in massive PE with hemodynamic instability, this patient''s blood pressure is stable. Option initiate warfarin therapy is incorrect because warfarin takes several days to achieve therapeutic anticoagulation and is not appropriate as initial therapy. Option perform an inferior vena cava filter placement is incorrect as this is typically reserved for patients with contraindications to anticoagulation or recurrent PE despite anticoagulation.'),'PE without hypotension but with hypoxia (SpO2 88%) is treated with immediate parenteral anticoagulation in hospital (ESC 2019). Normal RV function and troponin argue against thrombolysis, which is for shock. Warfarin cannot be started without parenteral bridging. An IVC filter is for contraindications to anticoagulation. Hypoxia rules out early discharge on apixaban.'),
('cmqgdhxby000h1yr8ok0d8hve','["Emergent upper endoscopy after resuscitation", "Oral proton pump inhibitor only", "CT abdomen before any endoscopy", "Discharge after normal saline bolus"]'::jsonb,'["Octreotide and ceftriaxone, then endoscopy within 12 hours", "Transfuse to hemoglobin above 10 g/dL, then endoscopy in 48 hours", "IV pantoprazole, then elective endoscopy in 48 hours", "CT angiography and IR embolization before endoscopy", "Balloon tamponade, then endoscopy within 24 hours"]'::jsonb,'Emergent upper endoscopy after resuscitation','Octreotide and ceftriaxone, then endoscopy within 12 hours','A',md5('A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis presents with hematemesis and melena. BP 82/50 mm Hg, HR 124/min. Hemoglobin 6.8 g/dL. After IV access and fluid resuscitation, he remains hypotensive.'),'A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis presents with hematemesis and melena. BP 82/50 mm Hg, HR 124/min. Hemoglobin 6.8 g/dL. Two large-bore IVs are placed and packed red cell transfusion has started.',md5('What is the most appropriate immediate intervention?'),'In addition to resuscitation, what is the most appropriate management?',md5('Hemodynamically unstable GI bleed in cirrhosis requires resuscitation and urgent endoscopy for variceal banding or sclerotherapy. PPI alone does not address variceal hemorrhage. CT delays definitive hemostasis.'),'Suspected variceal hemorrhage in cirrhosis: along with restrictive transfusion and resuscitation, start a vasoactive agent (octreotide) and antibiotic prophylaxis (ceftriaxone), then perform endoscopy within 12 hours (Baveno VII 2022; AASLD 2024 guidance). Balloon tamponade is only a bridge for uncontrolled bleeding. A PPI with delayed endoscopy is ulcer-bleed care and too slow. CT angiography and IR are not first-line for varices. Transfusing to Hgb >10 increases rebleeding (target 7-8 g/dL).'),
('cmqgdhz4a00101yr8bqaqfj2q','["Ceftriaxone IM plus doxycycline with metronidazole", "Azithromycin monotherapy", "Metronidazole alone", "Oral contraceptive pill"]'::jsonb,'["Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once plus azithromycin 1 g orally once, then repeated after 1 week", "Levofloxacin 500 mg daily and metronidazole 500 mg twice daily orally for 14 days", "Azithromycin 1 g orally once weekly for 2 weeks plus metronidazole 500 mg BID", "Metronidazole 500 mg orally twice daily for 7 days plus fluconazole 150 mg once", "Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once, doxycycline 100 mg BID, metronidazole 500 mg BID x14 days"]'::jsonb,'Ceftriaxone IM plus doxycycline with metronidazole','Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once, doxycycline 100 mg BID, metronidazole 500 mg BID x14 days','E',md5('A 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with lower abdominal pain, cervical motion tenderness, and mucopurulent discharge. Temp 38.1°C, WBC 13.2 × 10³/µL. Urine pregnancy test is negative.'),'A 24-year-old sexually active woman presents with lower abdominal pain, cervical motion tenderness, and mucopurulent discharge. Temp 38.1°C, WBC 13.2 × 10³/µL. Urine pregnancy test is negative.',md5('What is the most appropriate empiric treatment?'),'What is the most appropriate empiric treatment?',md5('PID requires coverage for gonorrhea, chlamydia, and anaerobes — ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole per CDC. Monotherapy regimens are inadequate for PID.'),'CDC 2021 STI guideline: outpatient PID is ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once plus doxycycline 100 mg orally BID and metronidazole 500 mg orally BID for 14 days. Why not: ceftriaxone with azithromycin is the outdated regimen, and azithromycin-based regimens lack evidence. Levofloxacin-based regimens are only for cephalosporin allergy with low gonorrhea risk. Metronidazole plus fluconazole treats vaginitis, not PID.'),
('cmqgdhzl600151yr8z85nd5i6','["Hold warfarin; consider oral vitamin K if bleeding persists", "Continue warfarin at same dose", "Fresh frozen plasma for all supratherapeutic INRs", "Protamine sulfate"]'::jsonb,'["Fresh frozen plasma and continue warfarin", "Continue warfarin and recheck INR in 1 week", "Hold warfarin and give 4-factor PCC", "Hold warfarin and give protamine sulfate", "Hold warfarin and give oral vitamin K"]'::jsonb,'Hold warfarin; consider oral vitamin K if bleeding persists','Hold warfarin and give oral vitamin K','E',md5('A 65-year-old man on warfarin for atrial fibrillation presents with nosebleeds and gum bleeding. INR 8.2. He is hemodynamically stable, Hgb 12.1 g/dL.'),'A 65-year-old man on warfarin for atrial fibrillation presents with a nosebleed that stopped with pressure and mild gum bleeding. INR 8.2. He is hemodynamically stable, Hgb 12.1 g/dL.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Supratherapeutic INR with minor bleeding — hold warfarin and give vitamin K if needed. FFP is for serious bleeding. Protamine reverses heparin, not warfarin.'),'Minor bleeding with supratherapeutic INR: hold warfarin and give oral vitamin K, 2.5-5 mg (ACC 2020 ECDP on bleeding with anticoagulants; CHEST). Why not: 4F-PCC is for major or life-threatening bleeding. FFP while continuing warfarin is wrong. Protamine reverses heparin. Simply continuing warfarin is unsafe.'),
('cmqghsngr000a1y91tsnvqvj5','["Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus", "Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state"]'::jsonb,'["Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Stress hyperglycemia", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus", "Steroid-induced hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb,'Type 1 diabetes mellitus','Type 1 diabetes mellitus','A',md5('A 10-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss. She has a history of asthma and is currently on albuterol as needed. Her blood glucose level is 450 mg/dL, and her urine is positive for ketones.'),'A 10-year-old girl presents to the emergency department with a 2-day history of polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss. She has a history of asthma and is currently on albuterol as needed. Her blood glucose level is 450 mg/dL, and her urine is positive for ketones.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The presentation of polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, and elevated blood glucose with ketonuria is classic for Type 1 diabetes mellitus, which is characterized by autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing beta cells.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s age, symptoms, and lab findings strongly indicate Type 1 diabetes, especially given the presence of ketones in the urine, which suggests diabetic ketoacidosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Type 2 diabetes mellitus: While Type 2 diabetes can present with similar symptoms, it is less common in this age group and typically presents with a more gradual onset and without ketones.
• Diabetes insipidus: Diabetes insipidus is characterized by polyuria and polydipsia but does not involve hyperglycemia or ketonuria.
• Hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state: This condition typically occurs in older adults with Type 2 diabetes and is associated with a higher level of dehydration and hyperosmolarity, not usually seen in children presenting acutely.'),'A 10-year-old with polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss, glucose 450 mg/dL and ketonuria has type 1 diabetes (ADA 2026; ISPAD 2022). Type 2 is less likely in a lean child with ketosis. Diabetes insipidus has no hyperglycemia. Inhaled albuterol does not produce steroid-induced hyperglycemia, and stress hyperglycemia needs an acute illness and does not cause weeks of osmotic symptoms.'),
('cmqght4xc00281y91zh2yygfv','["Lumbar herniated disc", "Lumbar strain", "Spinal stenosis", "Sacroiliac joint dysfunction"]'::jsonb,'["Lumbar strain", "Piriformis syndrome", "Spinal stenosis", "Sacroiliac joint dysfunction", "Lumbar herniated disc"]'::jsonb,'Lumbar herniated disc','Lumbar herniated disc','E',md5('A 60-year-old woman presents with lower back pain that radiates down her left leg. She reports numbness and tingling in her left foot. On examination, she has decreased sensation in the left L5 distribution and a positive straight leg raise test. There is no history of trauma.'),'A 60-year-old woman presents with lower back pain that radiates down her left leg. She reports numbness and tingling in her left foot. On examination, she has decreased sensation in the left L5 distribution and a positive straight leg raise test. There is no history of trauma.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The combination of radicular pain, sensory deficits, and a positive straight leg raise test is characteristic of a lumbar herniated disc, particularly affecting the L5 nerve root.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms and physical examination findings align well with a herniated disc, which commonly presents with nerve root compression.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Lumbar strain: This would typically present with localized pain without radicular symptoms or neurological deficits.
• Spinal stenosis: While this can cause similar symptoms, it usually presents with neurogenic claudication and is less likely to have a positive straight leg raise test.
• Sacroiliac joint dysfunction: This condition typically presents with pain localized to the sacroiliac joint area and does not typically cause radicular symptoms.'),'The combination of radicular pain, sensory deficits, and a positive straight leg raise test is characteristic of a lumbar herniated disc, particularly affecting the L5 nerve root.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms and physical examination findings align well with a herniated disc, which commonly presents with nerve root compression.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Lumbar strain: This would typically present with localized pain without radicular symptoms or neurological deficits.
• Spinal stenosis: While this can cause similar symptoms, it usually presents with neurogenic claudication and is less likely to have a positive straight leg raise test.
• Sacroiliac joint dysfunction: This condition typically presents with pain localized to the sacroiliac joint area and does not typically cause radicular symptoms.

Why not "Piriformis syndrome": A dermatomal L5 sensory deficit with a positive straight-leg raise points to radiculopathy, not piriformis entrapment.'),
('cmqgjphjt000l1y4vn078nnz4','["Prescribe topical corticosteroids", "Advise oral antihistamines", "Instruct to avoid scratching the rash", "Perform a skin biopsy"]'::jsonb,'["Advise oral antihistamines", "Prescribe topical corticosteroids", "Instruct to avoid scratching the rash", "Prescribe a 3-week oral prednisone taper", "Prescribe oral antibiotics"]'::jsonb,'Prescribe topical corticosteroids','Prescribe topical corticosteroids','B',md5('A 40-year-old male presents to the surgical clinic with a rash on his forearm that has been present for several weeks. The rash is erythematous with vesicles and crusting. He reports a recent camping trip where he was exposed to poison ivy.'),'A 40-year-old male presents to an urgent care clinic with a rash on his forearm that appeared 2 days ago. A 6 × 8 cm linear patch on one forearm (<5% BSA) is erythematous with vesicles and crusting; the face and genitals are spared. He reports a recent camping trip where he was exposed to poison ivy.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Topical corticosteroids are the first-line treatment for contact dermatitis caused by poison ivy, helping to reduce inflammation and itching.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s history of exposure to poison ivy and the description of the rash are consistent with contact dermatitis, warranting topical steroid treatment.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Advise oral antihistamines: While antihistamines can help with itching, they do not address the underlying inflammation as effectively as corticosteroids.
• Instruct to avoid scratching the rash: While this is good advice, it does not provide active treatment for the condition.
• Perform a skin biopsy: A biopsy is unnecessary for a typical case of contact dermatitis and would delay appropriate management.'),'Localized allergic contact dermatitis (<20% BSA, sparing the face and genitals): mid- to high-potency topical corticosteroid (AAD; AAFP 2022 contact dermatitis review). Why not: a 2-3 week systemic taper is for extensive or facial disease, and 5-day courses cause rebound. Antihistamines don''t treat the dermatitis. Avoiding scratching is adjunctive only. Antibiotics are not needed without infection.'),
('cmqgjpk2s001b1y4vozmkjrdi','["Transvaginal ultrasound", "Laparoscopy", "Urine pregnancy test", "IV fluids"]'::jsonb,'["Urine pregnancy test", "Transvaginal ultrasound", "Empiric treatment for PID", "Diagnostic laparoscopy", "Order a serum CA-125"]'::jsonb,'Urine pregnancy test','Urine pregnancy test','A',md5('A 22-year-old female presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding after a missed menstrual period. She has a history of irregular menstrual cycles and has been sexually active without contraception. A pelvic exam reveals cervical motion tenderness.'),'A 22-year-old female presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding after a missed menstrual period. She has a history of irregular menstrual cycles and has been sexually active without contraception. BP 118/76 mm Hg, HR 88/min. A pelvic exam reveals cervical motion tenderness.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('In a case of suspected ectopic pregnancy, the first step is to confirm pregnancy status with a urine pregnancy test, which will guide further management.

Clinical reasoning: Given the patient''s symptoms and history, confirming pregnancy is critical before proceeding with imaging or invasive procedures.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Transvaginal ultrasound: While this is important for evaluating ectopic pregnancy, it should only be done after confirming the patient is pregnant.
• Laparoscopy: This is an invasive procedure and should not be the first step without confirming pregnancy or assessing the need for surgical intervention.
• IV fluids: While IV fluids may be necessary if the patient is unstable, the immediate priority is to confirm pregnancy status.'),'A reproductive-age woman with a missed period, pain and bleeding needs a pregnancy test first to triage for ectopic pregnancy (ACOG Practice Bulletin 193). Transvaginal ultrasound is interpreted with the hCG result. Laparoscopy is for an unstable patient or a confirmed ectopic. CA-125 has no role. Empiric PID treatment before excluding pregnancy could miss an ectopic.'),
('cmqgjpgde000b1y4vbcthnd7f','["Inhibition of ovulation", "Thickening of cervical mucus", "Alteration of endometrial lining", "Suppression of FSH and LH"]'::jsonb,'["Increased prolactin secretion", "Direct spermicidal effect in the vagina", "Inhibition of ovulation", "Alteration of endometrial lining", "Thickening of cervical mucus"]'::jsonb,'Inhibition of ovulation','Inhibition of ovulation','C',md5('A 30-year-old woman presents for a routine check-up and expresses interest in birth control options. She has no significant medical history and does not smoke. On examination, her blood pressure is 120/80 mm Hg. She is concerned about the risk of thrombosis with hormonal contraceptives.'),'A 30-year-old woman presents for a routine check-up and expresses interest in birth control options. She has no significant medical history and does not smoke. On examination, her blood pressure is 120/80 mm Hg.',md5('What is the most likely mechanism by which combined oral contraceptives exert their effect?'),'What is the primary mechanism by which combined oral contraceptives prevent pregnancy?',md5('Combined oral contraceptives primarily work by inhibiting ovulation, preventing the release of eggs from the ovaries.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette presents a patient seeking contraceptive options, and understanding the mechanism of action of combined oral contraceptives is essential for patient education.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Thickening of cervical mucus: While this is a secondary mechanism, the primary action of combined oral contraceptives is to inhibit ovulation.
• Alteration of endometrial lining: This is also a secondary effect, but the main mechanism is ovulation inhibition.
• Suppression of FSH and LH: Although combined oral contraceptives do suppress these hormones, the direct result is the inhibition of ovulation, which is the primary mechanism.'),'Combined oral contraceptives work mainly by suppressing the midcycle gonadotropin surge, which inhibits ovulation (CDC US SPR 2024; ACOG). Cervical mucus thickening and endometrial changes are secondary progestin effects. Estrogen raises SHBG, not prolactin, and has no contraceptive role here. There is no spermicidal action.'),
('cmqgjph82000i1y4vxuz8je00','["Anterior shoulder dislocation", "Rotator cuff tear", "Fractured clavicle", "Shoulder impingement syndrome"]'::jsonb,'["Anterior shoulder dislocation", "Acromioclavicular separation", "Proximal humerus fracture", "Posterior shoulder dislocation", "Rotator cuff tear"]'::jsonb,'Anterior shoulder dislocation','Anterior shoulder dislocation','A',md5('A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with right shoulder pain after a fall while playing basketball. He has a history of recurrent shoulder dislocations. On examination, there is visible deformity and limited range of motion in the right shoulder.'),'A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department with right shoulder pain after a fall while playing basketball. On examination, the arm is held in slight abduction and external rotation, the deltoid contour is flattened, and range of motion is limited.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The mechanism of injury and clinical findings, including deformity and limited motion, are characteristic of an anterior shoulder dislocation.

Clinical reasoning: Given the history of recurrent dislocations and the acute presentation after trauma, anterior dislocation is the most likely diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Rotator cuff tear: This typically presents with weakness and pain but not with visible deformity.
• Fractured clavicle: While this can cause shoulder pain, it would not typically present with shoulder deformity alone.
• Shoulder impingement syndrome: This is a chronic condition and does not fit the acute presentation after trauma.'),'After a fall, an arm held in slight abduction and external rotation with a flattened deltoid contour is an anterior shoulder dislocation (about 95% of dislocations; AAOS). A posterior dislocation holds the arm adducted and internally rotated (seizure, shock). Rotator cuff tear and AC separation do not cause this deformity. A proximal humerus fracture shows bruising and crepitus in older patients and needs X-ray to exclude.'),
('cmqgjphgh000k1y4vdls4y778','["Prescribe acetaminophen and ibuprofen", "Recommend only acetaminophen", "Suggest only ibuprofen", "Order a prescription for opioids"]'::jsonb,'["Prescribe tramadol as needed for pain", "Prescribe oral hydrocodone with acetaminophen", "Recommend aspirin as needed", "Prescribe acetaminophen and ibuprofen", "Prescribe acetaminophen with codeine"]'::jsonb,'Prescribe acetaminophen and ibuprofen','Prescribe acetaminophen and ibuprofen','D',md5('A 10-year-old boy is scheduled for a tonsillectomy due to recurrent tonsillitis. During the pre-operative assessment, he reports a history of recurrent ear infections and has been treated with multiple courses of antibiotics. He has no known drug allergies. His mother asks about the best way to manage his pain post-operatively.'),'A 10-year-old boy is scheduled for a tonsillectomy due to recurrent tonsillitis. During the pre-operative assessment, he reports a history of recurrent ear infections and has been treated with multiple courses of antibiotics. He has no known drug allergies. His mother asks about the best way to manage his pain post-operatively.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management for post-operative pain control?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management for post-operative pain control?',md5('Combining acetaminophen and ibuprofen provides effective pain relief for children after tonsillectomy and is a common practice to manage post-operative pain.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette highlights the need for effective pain management in a pediatric patient undergoing tonsillectomy, where multimodal analgesia is beneficial.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Recommend only acetaminophen: While acetaminophen is effective, it may not provide sufficient pain relief on its own for post-operative tonsillectomy pain.
• Suggest only ibuprofen: Ibuprofen alone may not adequately control pain, especially in the post-operative setting where combination therapy is preferred.
• Order a prescription for opioids: Opioids are generally not first-line for pediatric post-operative pain due to risks of side effects and are reserved for more severe pain.'),'After tonsillectomy, acetaminophen and ibuprofen are recommended for pain (AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Tonsillectomy in Children 2019). Codeine and tramadol are contraindicated under age 12 and after pediatric tonsillectomy (FDA 2017 boxed warning). Routine opioids such as hydrocodone are avoided. Aspirin raises bleeding risk and Reye syndrome risk.'),
('cmqgjphqo000n1y4v5r5aiguz','["Administer systemic corticosteroids", "Increase the dose of the albuterol inhaler", "Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist", "Provide a spacer for the albuterol inhaler"]'::jsonb,'["Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist", "Add a leukotriene receptor antagonist", "Provide a spacer for the albuterol inhaler", "Increase the dose of the albuterol inhaler", "Administer systemic corticosteroids"]'::jsonb,'Administer systemic corticosteroids','Administer systemic corticosteroids','E',md5('A 25-year-old male with a history of asthma presents to the clinic with increased wheezing and shortness of breath over the past week. He reports using his albuterol inhaler multiple times a day. On examination, he has a respiratory rate of 24 breaths per minute and an oxygen saturation of 92% on room air.'),'A 25-year-old male with a history of asthma presents to the clinic with increased wheezing and shortness of breath over the past week. He reports using his albuterol inhaler multiple times a day. On examination, he has a respiratory rate of 24 breaths per minute and an oxygen saturation of 92% on room air.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Increased wheezing and shortness of breath with frequent use of a rescue inhaler indicate worsening asthma control, necessitating the use of systemic corticosteroids.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms suggest an exacerbation of asthma that is not adequately controlled with his current regimen, thus requiring a more aggressive treatment approach.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Increase the dose of the albuterol inhaler: Increasing the dose of a rescue inhaler is not appropriate in the setting of poor asthma control.
• Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist: Long-acting beta-agonists are not recommended as monotherapy in asthma and should be used with inhaled corticosteroids.
• Provide a spacer for the albuterol inhaler: While a spacer can improve inhaler technique, it does not address the acute exacerbation requiring systemic corticosteroids.'),'Increased wheezing and shortness of breath with frequent use of a rescue inhaler indicate worsening asthma control, necessitating the use of systemic corticosteroids.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms suggest an exacerbation of asthma that is not adequately controlled with his current regimen, thus requiring a more aggressive treatment approach.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Increase the dose of the albuterol inhaler: Increasing the dose of a rescue inhaler is not appropriate in the setting of poor asthma control.
• Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist: Long-acting beta-agonists are not recommended as monotherapy in asthma and should be used with inhaled corticosteroids.
• Provide a spacer for the albuterol inhaler: While a spacer can improve inhaler technique, it does not address the acute exacerbation requiring systemic corticosteroids.

Why not "Add a leukotriene receptor antagonist": Its onset is slow and it does not treat an acute exacerbation; systemic corticosteroids are indicated (GINA 2025).'),
('cmqgjpid0000t1y4v3v8z30ju','["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)", "Aplastic anemia", "Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)"]'::jsonb,'["Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)", "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "Aplastic anemia", "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)"]'::jsonb,'Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)','Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)','E',md5('A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric emergency department with a 2-day history of petechiae and easy bruising. His mother reports that he had a recent upper respiratory infection, and he is currently taking no medications. On examination, his vital signs are stable, but he has multiple petechiae on his trunk and extremities. A complete blood count reveals a platelet count of 30,000/mm³.'),'A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric emergency department with a 2-day history of petechiae and easy bruising. His mother reports that he had a recent upper respiratory infection, and he is currently taking no medications. On examination, his vital signs are stable, but he has multiple petechiae on his trunk and extremities. A complete blood count reveals a platelet count of 30,000/mm³.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The vignette describes a child with isolated thrombocytopenia and a recent viral infection, which is characteristic of ITP. ITP often follows viral illnesses in children and presents with petechiae and bruising due to low platelet counts.

Clinical reasoning: The recent upper respiratory infection suggests a post-viral etiology, aligning with ITP, which is common in this age group. The isolated low platelet count without other hematologic abnormalities supports this diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP): TTP typically presents with a pentad of symptoms including microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, neurological symptoms, renal dysfunction, and fever, which are not present here.
• Aplastic anemia: Aplastic anemia would present with pancytopenia (low red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets) and typically has a different clinical presentation, including fatigue and increased infections.
• Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS): HUS usually follows a gastrointestinal infection (often with E. coli) and presents with hemolytic anemia, acute renal failure, and thrombocytopenia, which are not indicated in this case.'),'The vignette describes a child with isolated thrombocytopenia and a recent viral infection, which is characteristic of ITP. ITP often follows viral illnesses in children and presents with petechiae and bruising due to low platelet counts.

Clinical reasoning: The recent upper respiratory infection suggests a post-viral etiology, aligning with ITP, which is common in this age group. The isolated low platelet count without other hematologic abnormalities supports this diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP): TTP typically presents with a pentad of symptoms including microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, neurological symptoms, renal dysfunction, and fever, which are not present here.
• Aplastic anemia: Aplastic anemia would present with pancytopenia (low red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets) and typically has a different clinical presentation, including fatigue and increased infections.
• Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS): HUS usually follows a gastrointestinal infection (often with E. coli) and presents with hemolytic anemia, acute renal failure, and thrombocytopenia, which are not indicated in this case.

Why not "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia": Thrombocytopenia is isolated after a viral illness, with no other cytopenia or organomegaly described.'),
('cmqglg1lt00031yqjhj1wweqi','["Administer prothrombin complex concentrate", "Perform a platelet transfusion", "Administer vitamin K", "Order a mixing study"]'::jsonb,'["Transfuse fresh frozen plasma empirically", "Perform a platelet transfusion", "Administer prothrombin complex concentrate", "Order a mixing study", "Administer vitamin K"]'::jsonb,'Order a mixing study','Order a mixing study','D',md5('A 45-year-old male patient is undergoing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During the procedure, he experiences significant bleeding. After the surgery, he is found to have a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). He has no prior history of bleeding disorders.'),'A 45-year-old male patient is undergoing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. During the procedure, he experiences significant bleeding. After the surgery, bleeding is controlled and he is hemodynamically stable, but he is found to have a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). PT/INR and platelet count are normal, he received no heparin, and he has no prior history of bleeding disorders.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('A mixing study is essential to differentiate between a factor deficiency and the presence of an inhibitor, especially given the prolonged aPTT in a previously healthy patient.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette suggests an acquired coagulopathy due to the surgical procedure, and the next step is to determine the underlying cause of the prolonged aPTT.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Administer prothrombin complex concentrate: This is used for urgent reversal of anticoagulation but does not address the need to identify the cause of the prolonged aPTT.
• Perform a platelet transfusion: Platelet transfusions are indicated for thrombocytopenia, not for prolonged aPTT without evidence of low platelet count.
• Administer vitamin K: Vitamin K is used for reversing warfarin effects but is not appropriate without knowing if the patient is on anticoagulation therapy.'),'Isolated prolonged aPTT with normal PT and platelets in a stable patient: a mixing study distinguishes factor deficiency from an inhibitor (ISTH/BSH guidance on aPTT evaluation). Why not: there is no active bleeding, so FFP and platelets aren''t indicated. PCC and vitamin K act on the PT/extrinsic pathway.'),
('cmqglg7pv001k1yqjmaw7w89q','["Perform a Pap smear and HPV testing", "Provide education on safe sex practices", "Screen for chlamydia and gonorrhea", "Administer the HPV vaccine"]'::jsonb,'["Administer the HPV vaccine", "Provide education on safe sex practices", "Order serologic HSV testing", "Screen for chlamydia and gonorrhea", "Perform a Pap smear and HPV testing"]'::jsonb,'Screen for chlamydia and gonorrhea','Screen for chlamydia and gonorrhea','D',md5('A 28-year-old sexually active woman visits her primary care provider for a routine check-up. She is concerned about her risk for sexually transmitted infections (STIs) as she has had multiple partners in the past year. She is currently asymptomatic and has no known exposure to STIs.'),'A 28-year-old sexually active woman visits her primary care provider for a routine check-up. She is concerned about her risk for sexually transmitted infections (STIs) as she has had multiple partners in the past year. She is currently asymptomatic and has no known exposure to STIs.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Given her sexual history, routine screening for chlamydia and gonorrhea is recommended for sexually active women under 25 years old, regardless of symptoms.

Clinical reasoning: Asymptomatic women with multiple partners are at increased risk for STIs, and early detection through screening can prevent complications.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Perform a Pap smear and HPV testing: Pap smears are recommended starting at age 21 and are not the immediate priority for STI screening.
• Provide education on safe sex practices: While important, education alone does not address the need for screening based on her risk factors.
• Administer the HPV vaccine: Vaccination is important but does not replace the need for STI screening in this context.'),'Given her sexual history, routine screening for chlamydia and gonorrhea is recommended for sexually active women under 25 years old, regardless of symptoms.

Clinical reasoning: Asymptomatic women with multiple partners are at increased risk for STIs, and early detection through screening can prevent complications.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Perform a Pap smear and HPV testing: Pap smears are recommended starting at age 21 and are not the immediate priority for STI screening.
• Provide education on safe sex practices: While important, education alone does not address the need for screening based on her risk factors.
• Administer the HPV vaccine: Vaccination is important but does not replace the need for STI screening in this context.

Why not "Order serologic HSV testing": USPSTF recommends against routine serologic HSV screening in asymptomatic people (USPSTF 2023).'),
('cmqgqknsk000k1ydw4hq51p81','["Topical corticosteroids", "Hydroxychloroquine", "Systemic corticosteroids", "Methotrexate"]'::jsonb,'["Hydroxychloroquine", "Topical corticosteroids", "Methotrexate", "Topical tacrolimus", "Systemic corticosteroids"]'::jsonb,'Hydroxychloroquine','Hydroxychloroquine','A',md5('A 40-year-old woman presents with a rash on her forearms and face that worsens with sun exposure. She reports joint pain and fatigue. Laboratory tests show a positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) and anti-double-stranded DNA antibody. A skin biopsy reveals interface dermatitis.'),'A 40-year-old woman presents with a rash on her forearms and face that worsens with sun exposure. She reports joint pain and fatigue. Laboratory tests show a positive antinuclear antibody (ANA) and anti-double-stranded DNA antibody. A skin biopsy reveals interface dermatitis.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Hydroxychloroquine is the first-line treatment for skin manifestations and systemic symptoms in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).

Clinical reasoning: The vignette describes a classic presentation of SLE with photosensitivity and positive autoantibodies, making hydroxychloroquine the most appropriate choice.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Topical corticosteroids: While they may help with localized skin symptoms, they are not sufficient for systemic disease management.
• Systemic corticosteroids: These may be used in severe cases but are not the first-line treatment for mild to moderate SLE.
• Methotrexate: This is typically reserved for more severe cases or specific manifestations of SLE, not as an initial treatment.'),'Hydroxychloroquine is the first-line treatment for skin manifestations and systemic symptoms in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).

Clinical reasoning: The vignette describes a classic presentation of SLE with photosensitivity and positive autoantibodies, making hydroxychloroquine the most appropriate choice.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Topical corticosteroids: While they may help with localized skin symptoms, they are not sufficient for systemic disease management.
• Systemic corticosteroids: These may be used in severe cases but are not the first-line treatment for mild to moderate SLE.
• Methotrexate: This is typically reserved for more severe cases or specific manifestations of SLE, not as an initial treatment.

Why not "Topical tacrolimus": Skin-directed therapy alone does not treat SLE with arthralgia and fatigue; hydroxychloroquine is recommended for all patients with SLE (EULAR 2023 SLE recommendations).'),
('cmqgqkp8800121ydwnzvwopgw','["Perform an HIV test", "Start pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP)", "Refer for counseling on safe sex practices", "Administer a hepatitis B vaccine"]'::jsonb,'["Order a urine drug screen", "Perform an HIV test", "Test for herpes simplex IgG", "Refer for counseling on safe sex practices", "Start pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP)"]'::jsonb,'Perform an HIV test','Perform an HIV test','B',md5('A 30-year-old man presents to a primary care clinic for routine screening. He is sexually active with multiple partners and has no significant medical history. He reports no symptoms but is concerned about sexually transmitted infections. His last HIV test was 2 years ago.'),'A 30-year-old man presents to a primary care clinic for routine screening. He is sexually active with multiple partners and has no significant medical history. He reports no symptoms but is concerned about sexually transmitted infections. His last HIV test was 2 years ago.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Given the patient''s high-risk sexual behavior and the fact that he has not been tested for HIV in the past 2 years, the most appropriate next step is to perform an HIV test to assess his current status.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette highlights the importance of regular HIV screening in sexually active individuals, particularly those with multiple partners, to ensure early detection and treatment.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Start pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP): PrEP is indicated for individuals at high risk of HIV but requires an initial HIV test to confirm negative status before starting.
• Refer for counseling on safe sex practices: While counseling is important, it does not address the immediate need for HIV testing given the patient''s risk factors.
• Administer a hepatitis B vaccine: Vaccination against hepatitis B is important but not the immediate priority compared to HIV testing in this scenario.'),'Sexually active adults with multiple partners should have HIV testing at least annually; his last test was 2 years ago (CDC 2006 recommendations, 2024 STI guidance). PrEP requires a documented negative HIV test first. Counseling is appropriate but does not replace testing. A urine drug screen is not indicated. HSV serology is not recommended for asymptomatic screening (USPSTF 2023).'),
('cmqgqv257000y1y4vlr9yr0st','["Mechanical low back pain", "Ankylosing spondylitis", "Herniated disc", "Lumbar strain"]'::jsonb,'["Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis", "Herniated disc", "Spinal stenosis", "Ankylosing spondylitis", "Mechanical low back pain"]'::jsonb,'Ankylosing spondylitis','Ankylosing spondylitis','D',md5('A 40-year-old male presents with lower back pain that has been worsening over the past three months. He describes the pain as dull and aching, with stiffness in the morning that improves with activity. There is no history of trauma. On examination, there is limited range of motion in the lumbar spine.'),'A 40-year-old male presents with lower back pain that has been worsening over the past three months. He describes the pain as dull and aching, with stiffness in the morning that improves with activity. There is no history of trauma. On examination, there is limited range of motion in the lumbar spine.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s age, morning stiffness that improves with activity, and limited range of motion suggest ankylosing spondylitis, a type of inflammatory arthritis affecting the spine.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette presents classic symptoms of ankylosing spondylitis, which is characterized by inflammatory back pain and stiffness.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Mechanical low back pain: Typically does not present with morning stiffness that improves with activity.
• Herniated disc: Usually presents with radicular symptoms and is often associated with a specific incident or trauma.
• Lumbar strain: Would typically be associated with a specific injury or activity and not chronic morning stiffness.'),'Insidious back pain in a man under 45, lasting more than 3 months, with morning stiffness that improves with activity and reduced lumbar motion, is inflammatory back pain from axial spondyloarthritis or ankylosing spondylitis (ASAS criteria; ACR/SAA/SPARTAN 2019). Mechanical pain and disc herniation worsen with activity and improve with rest. DISH affects older adults and spares the SI joints. Spinal stenosis gives neurogenic claudication in older adults.'),
('cmqgqv5s8001y1y4vx16p403s','["Benign prostatic hyperplasia", "Prostate cancer", "Diabetes insipidus", "Urinary tract infection"]'::jsonb,'["Nocturnal polyuria from evening fluid intake", "Chronic bacterial prostatitis", "Benign prostatic hyperplasia", "Prostate cancer", "Overactive bladder"]'::jsonb,'Benign prostatic hyperplasia','Benign prostatic hyperplasia','C',md5('A 50-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of frequent urination, especially at night. He has a history of diabetes and hypertension. A urinalysis shows no signs of infection, and a prostate exam reveals an enlarged prostate.'),'A 50-year-old male presents to the clinic with frequent urination, nocturia, weak stream, hesitancy and post-void dribbling. He has a history of diabetes and hypertension. His HbA1c is 6.4%, and a urinalysis shows no signs of infection, and the prostate is smooth, symmetric and nontender without nodules.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms of nocturia and an enlarged prostate on examination are characteristic of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).

Clinical reasoning: BPH is common in older men and leads to urinary symptoms due to bladder outlet obstruction.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Prostate cancer: While prostate cancer can present similarly, it typically has additional symptoms such as weight loss or bone pain.
• Diabetes insipidus: This condition causes excessive urination due to a deficiency of ADH, but the urinalysis does not support this diagnosis.
• Urinary tract infection: The urinalysis shows no signs of infection, making this diagnosis unlikely.'),'Obstructive LUTS (weak stream, hesitancy, dribbling) in a man with a smooth, symmetric, nontender prostate suggest BPH (AUA 2021 BPH guideline). Why not: nocturnal polyuria from evening fluids doesn''t explain obstructive symptoms. Chronic bacterial prostatitis causes pain and recurrent UTIs with a tender gland, and his UA is negative. Diabetes is controlled (HbA1c 6.4%). Overactive bladder lacks obstruction. Prostate cancer is usually nodular or asymmetric and often asymptomatic (AUA/SUO 2023).'),
('cmqgqv0z4000n1y4vxaczmw92','["Stable angina", "Unstable angina", "Myocardial infarction", "Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb,'["Myocardial infarction", "Unstable angina", "Prinzmetal angina", "Aortic stenosis", "Stable angina"]'::jsonb,'Stable angina','Stable angina','E',md5('A 62-year-old woman with a history of hyperlipidemia and hypertension presents to the clinic for a routine check-up. She reports occasional chest discomfort during physical exertion, which resolves with rest. Her blood pressure is 130/80 mm Hg, and her lipid panel shows an LDL of 160 mg/dL. She is a current smoker and has a family history of coronary artery disease.'),'A 62-year-old woman with a history of hyperlipidemia and hypertension presents to the clinic for a routine check-up. She reports occasional chest discomfort during physical exertion, which resolves with rest. Her blood pressure is 130/80 mm Hg, and her lipid panel shows an LDL of 160 mg/dL. She is a current smoker and has a family history of coronary artery disease.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms of chest discomfort during exertion that resolve with rest, combined with her risk factors, suggest stable angina. This condition is characterized by predictable chest pain due to myocardial ischemia during physical activity.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette indicates exertional chest discomfort that resolves with rest, which is classic for stable angina. The patient''s risk factors, including smoking and family history, further support this diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Unstable angina: This condition typically presents with chest pain that occurs at rest or is of increased frequency or intensity, which is not described here.
• Myocardial infarction: A myocardial infarction would present with more severe, persistent chest pain, often accompanied by other symptoms such as diaphoresis or nausea.
• Aortic stenosis: While aortic stenosis can cause exertional symptoms, the classic presentation involves a triad of exertional dyspnea, syncope, and angina, which is not indicated in this vignette.'),'The patient''s symptoms of chest discomfort during exertion that resolve with rest, combined with her risk factors, suggest stable angina. This condition is characterized by predictable chest pain due to myocardial ischemia during physical activity.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette indicates exertional chest discomfort that resolves with rest, which is classic for stable angina. The patient''s risk factors, including smoking and family history, further support this diagnosis.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Unstable angina: This condition typically presents with chest pain that occurs at rest or is of increased frequency or intensity, which is not described here.
• Myocardial infarction: A myocardial infarction would present with more severe, persistent chest pain, often accompanied by other symptoms such as diaphoresis or nausea.
• Aortic stenosis: While aortic stenosis can cause exertional symptoms, the classic presentation involves a triad of exertional dyspnea, syncope, and angina, which is not indicated in this vignette.

Why not "Prinzmetal angina": Vasospastic angina typically occurs at rest; this pain is exertional and relieved by rest.'),
('cmqgr7uta000n1y8romy4cszx','["Acute exacerbation of asthma", "Pneumonia", "Bronchiolitis", "Anaphylaxis"]'::jsonb,'["Bronchiolitis", "Community-acquired pneumonia", "Acute exacerbation of asthma", "Anaphylaxis", "Foreign body aspiration"]'::jsonb,'Acute exacerbation of asthma','Acute exacerbation of asthma','C',md5('A 10-year-old boy with a history of asthma presents to the emergency department with difficulty breathing and wheezing after playing outside. He has been using his albuterol inhaler every 2 hours for the past day. On examination, his respiratory rate is 30 breaths/min, and he has an oxygen saturation of 92% on room air.'),'A 10-year-old boy presents to the emergency department with difficulty breathing and wheezing after playing outside. He has had two similar episodes this year, and his symptoms began after exposure to cut grass. Diffuse bilateral expiratory wheezes are heard. On examination, his respiratory rate is 30 breaths/min, and he has an oxygen saturation of 92% on room air.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient’s history of asthma, frequent use of his rescue inhaler, and wheezing upon examination indicate an acute exacerbation of asthma. This condition is characterized by increased airway inflammation and bronchoconstriction, leading to difficulty breathing.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette highlights the patient''s known asthma and the acute worsening of symptoms after exertion, which is typical for an asthma exacerbation.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Pneumonia: While pneumonia can cause wheezing, the acute onset after physical activity and the patient''s history of asthma strongly suggest an exacerbation rather than an infection.
• Bronchiolitis: Bronchiolitis primarily affects infants and young children and is characterized by a viral infection causing wheezing; this patient is older and has a known history of asthma.
• Anaphylaxis: Anaphylaxis would typically present with additional symptoms such as hives, swelling, or gastrointestinal distress, which are not mentioned in this vignette.'),'Recurrent episodes of wheeze and dyspnea triggered by allergen exposure, with diffuse bilateral expiratory wheezes and hypoxia, are an acute asthma exacerbation (GINA 2025). Community-acquired pneumonia has fever and focal findings. Bronchiolitis affects children under 2. Anaphylaxis involves skin, GI or circulatory signs. Foreign body aspiration causes unilateral, sudden findings.'),
('cmqgr7x0k00191y8ryf3e3w8l','["Renal calculi", "Pyelonephritis", "Cystitis", "Glomerulonephritis"]'::jsonb,'["Pyelonephritis", "Ureteropelvic junction obstruction", "Obstructing ureteral stone", "Renal infarction", "Cystitis"]'::jsonb,'Renal calculi','Obstructing ureteral stone','C',md5('A 34-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with hematuria and flank pain. She has a history of recurrent urinary tract infections. A CT scan reveals a 2 cm renal stone in the left kidney.'),'A 34-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with hematuria and flank pain. She has a history of recurrent urinary tract infections. She is afebrile, and urinalysis shows red blood cells without nitrites or leukocyte esterase. Noncontrast CT shows mild left hydronephrosis with a 6-mm hyperdense focus at the left ureterovesical junction.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms of flank pain and hematuria, along with imaging findings, are characteristic of renal calculi.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette provides clear evidence of a renal stone, which explains the acute presentation.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Pyelonephritis: This condition would typically present with fever and systemic symptoms, which are not noted here.
• Cystitis: Cystitis usually presents with dysuria and urgency rather than flank pain.
• Glomerulonephritis: This condition often presents with different symptoms, including edema and hypertension, rather than acute flank pain.'),'Flank pain and hematuria with hydronephrosis and a hyperdense focus at the ureterovesical junction on noncontrast CT is an obstructing ureteral stone (AUA/Endourological Society Medical Management of Kidney Stones 2016). Pyelonephritis and cystitis are excluded by the afebrile course and a urinalysis negative for infection. Renal infarction causes flank pain with a high LDH and wedge-shaped perfusion defects. UPJ obstruction is a congenital narrowing without a calcification at the UVJ.'),
('cmqgr80g000281y8rxpaaz1st','["Refer for dialysis evaluation", "Increase lisinopril dosage", "Start erythropoietin therapy", "Continue current management and recheck labs in 3 months"]'::jsonb,'["Increase lisinopril dosage", "Refer to nephrology for CKD management", "Stop lisinopril immediately", "Continue current management and recheck labs in 3 months", "Start erythropoietin therapy"]'::jsonb,'Refer for dialysis evaluation','Refer to nephrology for CKD management','B',md5('A 50-year-old male with chronic kidney disease stage 4 presents to the clinic for evaluation of his renal function. He is currently on a low-protein diet and takes lisinopril for hypertension. His most recent labs show a creatinine of 3.2 mg/dL and a GFR of 25 mL/min.'),'A 50-year-old male with chronic kidney disease stage 4 presents to the clinic for evaluation of his renal function. He is currently on a low-protein diet and takes lisinopril for hypertension. His most recent labs show a creatinine of 3.2 mg/dL and an eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73 m², potassium 4.8 mEq/L, and hemoglobin 12.4 g/dL. He has no uremic symptoms.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Given the patient''s stage 4 chronic kidney disease and declining renal function, referral for dialysis evaluation is warranted.

Clinical reasoning: With a GFR of 25 mL/min, the patient is approaching the need for renal replacement therapy, making a referral appropriate.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Increase lisinopril dosage: Increasing lisinopril may not be appropriate given the patient''s declining renal function and potential for further renal impairment.
• Start erythropoietin therapy: While anemia management is important, it does not address the immediate need for renal replacement therapy.
• Continue current management and recheck labs in 3 months: This approach is inadequate given the patient''s significant renal impairment and need for timely intervention.'),'KDIGO 2024: refer to nephrology at eGFR <30 (G4) for RRT planning and complication management. Why not: lisinopril should be continued, not stopped or escalated, with K 4.8. Watchful waiting delays planning. Erythropoietin is not indicated at Hgb 12.4.'),
('cmqgtre03000d1yd2j1c4f92s','["Major depressive disorder", "Generalized anxiety disorder", "Bipolar disorder", "Adjustment disorder"]'::jsonb,'["Somatic symptom disorder", "Generalized anxiety disorder", "Bipolar disorder", "Major depressive disorder", "Adjustment disorder"]'::jsonb,'Major depressive disorder','Major depressive disorder','D',md5('A 34-year-old woman presents to the clinic with persistent low mood, lack of interest in activities she once enjoyed, and difficulty concentrating for the past 6 months. She reports significant weight loss and insomnia. Her past medical history is unremarkable, and she is not taking any medications. On examination, she appears disheveled and has a flat affect.'),'A 34-year-old woman presents to the clinic with persistent low mood, lack of interest in activities she once enjoyed, and difficulty concentrating for the past 6 months. She reports significant weight loss and insomnia. Her past medical history is unremarkable, and she is not taking any medications. On examination, she appears disheveled and has a flat affect.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The patient''s symptoms, including persistent low mood, anhedonia, weight loss, and insomnia, align with the criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by a depressed mood lasting at least two weeks, accompanied by other symptoms such as changes in appetite, sleep disturbances, and cognitive difficulties. Generalized anxiety disorder is primarily characterized by excessive worry rather than a depressed mood. Bipolar disorder involves episodes of mania or hypomania, which are not present here. Adjustment disorder typically occurs in response to a specific stressor and does not meet the duration criteria for MDD.'),'The patient''s symptoms, including persistent low mood, anhedonia, weight loss, and insomnia, align with the criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD). MDD is characterized by a depressed mood lasting at least two weeks, accompanied by other symptoms such as changes in appetite, sleep disturbances, and cognitive difficulties. Generalized anxiety disorder is primarily characterized by excessive worry rather than a depressed mood. Bipolar disorder involves episodes of mania or hypomania, which are not present here. Adjustment disorder typically occurs in response to a specific stressor and does not meet the duration criteria for MDD.

Why not "Somatic symptom disorder": Anhedonia, low mood, weight loss and insomnia are core depressive symptoms, not a focus on somatic symptoms.'),
('cmqgtre7y000g1yd2s1kkj8ln','["Administer desmopressin", "Transfusion of fresh frozen plasma", "Surgical ligation of bleeding vessels", "Administer vitamin K"]'::jsonb,'["Administer desmopressin", "Transfusion of fresh frozen plasma", "Administer vitamin K", "Transfuse platelets", "Surgical ligation of bleeding vessels"]'::jsonb,'Administer desmopressin','Administer desmopressin','A',md5('A 32-year-old woman is undergoing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones. During the procedure, she begins to bleed excessively. Her medical history is significant for von Willebrand disease. The surgical team is concerned about her bleeding risk.'),'A 32-year-old woman is undergoing a laparoscopic cholecystectomy for symptomatic gallstones. During the procedure, there is diffuse oozing from the operative bed without a discrete bleeding vessel. She has type 1 von Willebrand disease with a documented response to a desmopressin trial. The surgical team is concerned about her bleeding risk.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('In patients with von Willebrand disease, desmopressin is often used to increase levels of von Willebrand factor and factor VIII, which can help control bleeding during surgical procedures. Fresh frozen plasma can be considered but is not the first-line treatment for this specific bleeding disorder. Surgical ligation may be necessary if the bleeding is from a specific vessel, but it does not address the underlying coagulopathy. Vitamin K is not effective for von Willebrand disease as it primarily affects the vitamin K-dependent clotting factors.'),'In type 1 von Willebrand disease with a documented desmopressin response, diffuse surgical oozing is treated with desmopressin, which releases endogenous VWF and factor VIII (ASH/ISTH/NHF/WFH 2021 VWD management guidelines). VWF concentrate is for nonresponders or types 2B and 3. FFP is a poor VWF source. Ligation is for a discrete bleeding vessel, and none is present. Vitamin K does not affect VWF. Platelets are not indicated with a normal count.'),
('cmqgz4dmu00401ybc9o1wv0qi','["Airway hyperresponsiveness", "Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy", "Increased mucus production", "Airway remodeling"]'::jsonb,'["Ciliary dysfunction", "Airway hyperresponsiveness", "Alveolar wall destruction", "Defective CFTR chloride transport", "Bronchiolitis obliterans"]'::jsonb,'Airway hyperresponsiveness','Airway hyperresponsiveness','B',md5('A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic with a history of recurrent respiratory infections and wheezing. His mother reports that he often has difficulty breathing during physical activities. Vital signs show BP 100/60 mm Hg, HR 110/min, and SpO₂ 92% on room air. A family history of asthma is noted.'),'A 5-year-old boy presents to the pediatric clinic with recurrent episodes of wheezing and cough triggered by colds and exercise that resolve between episodes. His mother reports that he often has difficulty breathing during physical activities. Growth is normal, and wheezing improves after albuterol. A family history of asthma is noted.',md5('What is the most likely mechanism responsible for this child''s condition?'),'What is the most likely mechanism responsible for this child''s condition?',md5('The correct answer is airway hyperresponsiveness because this child''s symptoms of wheezing and difficulty breathing during physical activity, combined with a family history of asthma, suggest that he has asthma characterized by increased sensitivity of the airways to various stimuli. This hyperresponsiveness leads to bronchoconstriction and airflow limitation. 

Option bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy is incorrect because while it can occur in chronic asthma, it is a consequence rather than the primary mechanism causing the acute symptoms. 

Option increased mucus production is incorrect because while it is a feature of asthma, it is not the primary mechanism responsible for the child''s symptoms of wheezing and difficulty breathing. 

Option airway remodeling is incorrect because it refers to long-term structural changes that occur in chronic asthma, but the immediate mechanism causing the child''s symptoms is airway hyperresponsiveness.'),'Episodic wheeze and cough triggered by viral colds and exercise, resolving between episodes and responding to albuterol, with a family history of asthma, reflect airway hyperresponsiveness (GINA 2025). Alveolar destruction is emphysema. Ciliary dysfunction (primary ciliary dyskinesia) gives a chronic wet cough, sinusitis and situs abnormalities. CFTR defects (cystic fibrosis) cause failure to thrive and steatorrhea. Bronchiolitis obliterans gives fixed obstruction after severe infection, without bronchodilator response.'),
('cmqgz630p000x1yqym1jj1way','["Acute pancreatitis due to gallstones", "Acute pancreatitis due to alcohol use", "Cholecystitis", "Peptic ulcer disease"]'::jsonb,'["Pancreatitis due to hypertriglyceridemia", "Acute pancreatitis due to gallstones", "Pancreatitis due to hypercalcemia", "Acute pancreatitis due to alcohol use", "Peptic ulcer disease"]'::jsonb,'Acute pancreatitis due to alcohol use','Acute pancreatitis due to alcohol use','D',md5('A 50-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting after a heavy meal. He has a history of alcohol use disorder. On examination, he has a tender abdomen with guarding. Serum amylase is 450 U/L and lipase is 600 U/L.'),'A 50-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting after a weekend of heavy drinking. He has a history of alcohol use disorder. On examination, he has a tender abdomen with guarding. Serum amylase is 450 U/L and lipase is 600 U/L. ALT is 38 U/L; calcium and triglycerides are normal. Right upper quadrant ultrasound shows no gallstones and a normal bile duct.',md5('What is the best explanation for the findings?'),'What is the best explanation for the findings?',md5('The correct answer is acute pancreatitis due to alcohol use, as the patient''s history of alcohol use disorder and elevated serum lipase levels strongly suggest this diagnosis. Option acute pancreatitis due to gallstones is incorrect because there is no mention of gallstone history or biliary symptoms. Option cholecystitis is incorrect because it typically presents with right upper quadrant pain and may have a positive Murphy''s sign, which is not indicated here. Option peptic ulcer disease is incorrect as it would present differently, typically with epigastric pain and possibly hematemesis, not the acute severe pain and elevated lipase seen in this case.'),'Lipase over 3 times the upper limit after binge drinking, with no gallstones, a normal bile duct, normal ALT and normal calcium and triglycerides, points to alcohol-related acute pancreatitis (ACG acute pancreatitis guideline 2024). Gallstone pancreatitis is suggested by stones or duct dilation and an ALT >150 U/L. Hypertriglyceridemia (TG >1,000 mg/dL) and hypercalcemia are excluded by the labs. Peptic ulcer disease does not raise lipase this much.'),
('cmqgdhvhz00001yr8ngjgwd40','["Administer sublingual nitroglycerin and observe in the ED", "Activate the cath lab for primary PCI", "Order a troponin and repeat ECG in 6 hours", "Start heparin infusion and schedule stress test tomorrow"]'::jsonb,'["Sublingual nitroglycerin and observe in the ED", "IV metoprolol and repeat ECG in 1 hour", "Give fibrinolytics in the ED, then transfer in 24 hours", "Troponin and repeat ECG in 6 hours", "Activate the cath lab for primary PCI"]'::jsonb,'Activate the cath lab for primary PCI','Activate the cath lab for primary PCI','E',md5('A 58-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents with 45 minutes of substernal pressure radiating to the left arm. He is diaphoretic. BP 148/92 mm Hg, HR 96/min, SpO₂ 97% on room air. ECG shows 2 mm ST elevation in leads V2–V4.'),'A 58-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents to a PCI-capable hospital with 45 minutes of substernal pressure radiating to the left arm. He is diaphoretic. BP 148/92 mm Hg, HR 96/min, SpO₂ 97% on room air. ECG shows 2 mm ST elevation in leads V2–V4.',md5('Which is the most appropriate immediate management?'),'Which is the most appropriate immediate management?',md5('Anterior STEMI with ongoing symptoms requires emergent reperfusion — primary PCI when available within guideline time frames. Nitroglycerin alone does not reperfuse occluded coronary arteries. Delaying reperfusion increases infarct size and mortality.'),'Anterior STEMI at a PCI-capable hospital within 12 hours of symptom onset: activate the cath lab for primary PCI, with a door-to-device goal of 90 minutes or less (2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI ACS guideline). Fibrinolysis is for when PCI cannot be done within 120 minutes. Nitroglycerin with observation, or waiting for troponin, delays reperfusion. IV metoprolol is not reperfusion therapy and can precipitate shock.'),
('cmqgdhvn200011yr8wf38gisq','["Azithromycin monotherapy", "Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin", "Amoxicillin-clavulanate monotherapy", "Vancomycin monotherapy"]'::jsonb,'["IV azithromycin daily", "Oral doxycycline for 7 days", "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin", "Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin", "Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole"]'::jsonb,'Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin','Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin','D',md5('A 72-year-old nursing home resident with COPD presents with fever, productive cough, and confusion. Temp 38.9°C, RR 28/min, BP 92/58 mm Hg, SpO₂ 88% on room air. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe infiltrate.'),'A 72-year-old nursing home resident with COPD presents with fever, productive cough, and confusion. Temp 38.9°C, RR 28/min, BP 92/58 mm Hg, SpO₂ 88% on room air. Chest X-ray shows right lower lobe infiltrate.',md5('Which empiric antibiotic regimen is most appropriate?'),'Which empiric antibiotic regimen is most appropriate?',md5('This patient has severe CAP with hypotension and hypoxemia requiring hospitalization. IDSA/ATS severe CAP regimens include a beta-lactam plus macrolide (or respiratory fluoroquinolone). Azithromycin alone is insufficient for severe disease. Vancomycin is not first-line for uncomplicated CAP without MRSA risk.'),'This patient has severe CAP with hypotension and hypoxemia requiring hospitalization. IDSA/ATS severe CAP regimens include a beta-lactam plus macrolide (or respiratory fluoroquinolone). Azithromycin alone is insufficient for severe disease. Vancomycin is not first-line for uncomplicated CAP without MRSA risk.

Why not "IV azithromycin daily": macrolide monotherapy is not adequate for severe CAP (ATS/IDSA 2019).
Why not "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate plus azithromycin": this is an outpatient regimen; hypotension, confusion and hypoxia mean severe inpatient CAP needing IV beta-lactam therapy.
Why not "Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole": metronidazole does not cover atypical organisms, and anaerobic coverage is not routinely added (ATS/IDSA 2019).
Why not "Oral doxycycline for 7 days": this is outpatient therapy for healthy adults.'),
('cmqgdhvtz00031yr8h4h10sk0','["Start aspirin 325 mg and admit to stroke unit", "Administer IV alteplase if no contraindications", "Begin heparin drip immediately", "Obtain MRI before any treatment"]'::jsonb,'["Start aspirin 325 mg and admit to the stroke unit", "Lower BP to under 140/90 before alteplase", "Administer IV alteplase if no contraindications", "Obtain MRI brain before treatment", "Begin heparin drip immediately"]'::jsonb,'Administer IV alteplase if no contraindications','Administer IV alteplase if no contraindications','C',md5('A 67-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation (not anticoagulated) develops sudden right-sided weakness and aphasia. Last known well 90 minutes ago. BP 168/92 mm Hg, glucose 118 mg/dL, NIHSS 14. CT head shows no hemorrhage.'),'A 67-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation (not anticoagulated) develops sudden right-sided weakness and aphasia. Last known well 90 minutes ago. BP 168/92 mm Hg, glucose 118 mg/dL, NIHSS 14. CT head shows no hemorrhage.',md5('Which management is most appropriate?'),'Which management is most appropriate?',md5('Acute ischemic stroke within the tPA window without hemorrhage on CT warrants thrombolysis when eligible. Aspirin alone misses reperfusion opportunity. Immediate anticoagulation is not indicated in acute stroke. Non-contrast CT excludes hemorrhage — MRI should not delay treatment.'),'Acute ischemic stroke within the tPA window without hemorrhage on CT warrants thrombolysis when eligible. Aspirin alone misses reperfusion opportunity. Immediate anticoagulation is not indicated in acute stroke. Non-contrast CT excludes hemorrhage — MRI should not delay treatment.

Why not "Start aspirin 325 mg and admit to the stroke unit": aspirin is delayed 24 hours after thrombolysis and is not reperfusion therapy.
Why not "Obtain MRI brain before treatment": a noncontrast CT is sufficient, and MRI delays thrombolysis (AHA/ASA acute ischemic stroke guideline).
Why not "Lower BP to under 140/90 before alteplase": the threshold is <185/110 mm Hg; 168/92 already qualifies.'),
('cmqgdhwg500081yr8ezkgmr4k','["Ventilation-perfusion scan", "CT pulmonary angiography", "Lower extremity Doppler only", "Repeat D-dimer in 1 week"]'::jsonb,'["CT pulmonary angiography", "Echocardiogram to look for RV strain", "MRI pulmonary angiography", "Repeat leg ultrasound in 1 week", "Ventilation-perfusion scan"]'::jsonb,'Ventilation-perfusion scan','Ventilation-perfusion scan','E',md5('A 28-year-old woman at 32 weeks gestation presents with sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. HR 118/min, RR 26/min, BP 102/68 mm Hg, SpO₂ 93% on room air. Exam is notable for tachycardia and clear lungs. D-dimer is elevated; leg ultrasound shows no DVT.'),'A 28-year-old woman at 32 weeks gestation presents with sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. HR 118/min, RR 26/min, BP 102/68 mm Hg, SpO₂ 93% on room air. Exam is notable for tachycardia and clear lungs. D-dimer is elevated; leg ultrasound shows no DVT, and chest radiograph is normal. She had anaphylaxis to iodinated contrast 2 years ago.',md5('What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?'),'What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?',md5('In pregnancy, V/Q scan is often preferred over CTPA when chest X-ray is normal to minimize fetal radiation exposure. Clinical suspicion for PE is high; repeating D-dimer or Doppler alone is insufficient. CTPA is used when V/Q is nondiagnostic or contraindicated.'),'For suspected PE in pregnancy with a negative leg ultrasound and a normal chest radiograph, V/Q scanning is preferred (ASH 2018 VTE guideline; ATS/STR 2011). Her contrast anaphylaxis also makes CTPA inappropriate. Repeating leg ultrasound delays diagnosis. Echocardiography cannot exclude PE. MR pulmonary angiography is not validated in pregnancy and is not recommended.'),
('cmqgdhwyf000d1yr8lu8n4ktp','["CT pulmonary angiography", "Ventilation-perfusion scan only without further imaging", "Lower extremity ultrasound and discharge if negative", "Start warfarin without imaging"]'::jsonb,'["Repeat D-dimer in 24 hours", "Echocardiogram to assess the right ventricle", "Lower extremity ultrasound and discharge if negative", "CT pulmonary angiography", "Empiric apixaban and outpatient CT in 1 week"]'::jsonb,'CT pulmonary angiography','CT pulmonary angiography','D',md5('A 40-year-old woman presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after a 12-hour flight. HR 104/min, RR 22/min, BP 118/70 mm Hg, SpO₂ 94%. Wells score is moderate. D-dimer is elevated.'),'A 40-year-old woman presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after a 12-hour flight. HR 104/min, RR 22/min, BP 118/70 mm Hg, SpO₂ 94%. Wells score is moderate. D-dimer is elevated.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step?'),'What is the most appropriate next step?',md5('Moderate-to-high pretest probability with elevated D-dimer warrants definitive imaging — CTPA is standard in non-pregnant adults. V/Q alone without proceeding based on result is incomplete. Treating without imaging risks anticoagulating without diagnosis. Negative leg US does not exclude PE.'),'Moderate-to-high pretest probability with elevated D-dimer warrants definitive imaging — CTPA is standard in non-pregnant adults. V/Q alone without proceeding based on result is incomplete. Treating without imaging risks anticoagulating without diagnosis. Negative leg US does not exclude PE.

Why not "Empiric apixaban and outpatient CT in 1 week": a symptomatic, hypoxic patient needs same-day imaging.
Why not "Repeat D-dimer in 24 hours": it is already elevated, and repeating it adds nothing.
Why not "Echocardiogram to assess the right ventricle": echo cannot exclude PE in a stable patient (ESC 2019).'),
('cmqgdhx54000f1yr8amb7o2hk','["Acute pancreatitis", "Acute cholecystitis", "Peptic ulcer perforation", "Mesenteric ischemia"]'::jsonb,'["Acute cholecystitis", "Mesenteric ischemia", "Alcoholic hepatitis", "Peptic ulcer perforation", "Acute pancreatitis"]'::jsonb,'Acute pancreatitis','Acute pancreatitis','E',md5('A 52-year-old man with a history of heavy alcohol use presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back for 24 hours. Temp 38.4°C, HR 112/min, BP 98/60 mm Hg. Abdomen is tender epigastrically without peritoneal signs. Lipase 1,840 U/L (normal <60), AST 210 U/L, ALT 98 U/L, WBC 16.2 × 10³/µL.'),'A 52-year-old man with a history of heavy alcohol use presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back for 24 hours. Temp 38.4°C, HR 112/min, BP 98/60 mm Hg. Abdomen is tender epigastrically without peritoneal signs. Lipase 1,840 U/L (normal <60), AST 210 U/L, ALT 98 U/L, WBC 16.2 × 10³/µL.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('Epigastric pain radiating to the back with lipase >3× ULN in a patient with alcohol use is acute pancreatitis. Cholecystitis typically presents with RUQ pain and Murphy sign. Perforated ulcer would show free air and peritoneal signs. Mesenteric ischemia presents with pain out of proportion to exam.'),'Epigastric pain radiating to the back with lipase >3× ULN in a patient with alcohol use is acute pancreatitis. Cholecystitis typically presents with RUQ pain and Murphy sign. Perforated ulcer would show free air and peritoneal signs. Mesenteric ischemia presents with pain out of proportion to exam.

Why not "Alcoholic hepatitis": Lipase >3 times normal with epigastric pain radiating to the back meets the criteria for acute pancreatitis (ACG 2024 acute pancreatitis guideline).'),
('cmqgdhxw5000n1yr8md0s7w6l','["Blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and empiric IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin", "Oral amoxicillin and outpatient follow-up", "CT head only without antibiotics", "Acyclovir monotherapy"]'::jsonb,'["Blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and IV ampicillin plus IV gentamicin", "Blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin", "Dexamethasone first, then antibiotics after MRI of the brain", "Head CT, then lumbar puncture and antibiotics if CT is normal", "IV acyclovir and lumbar puncture once the fever resolves"]'::jsonb,'Blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and empiric IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin','Blood cultures, lumbar puncture, and IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin','B',md5('A 30-year-old man who injects drugs presents with fever, neck stiffness, and photophobia. Temp 39.5°C, HR 118/min, BP 110/68 mm Hg. Kernig and Brudzinski signs are positive. WBC 18 × 10³/µL.'),'A 30-year-old man who injects drugs presents with fever, neck stiffness, and photophobia. Temp 39.5°C, HR 118/min, BP 110/68 mm Hg. Kernig and Brudzinski signs are positive. WBC 18 × 10³/µL.',md5('What is the most appropriate initial management?'),'What is the most appropriate initial management?',md5('Suspected bacterial meningitis requires immediate blood cultures, LP (when safe), and empiric IV antibiotics covering S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis — do not delay for CT unless focal neuro signs. Outpatient oral therapy is unsafe.'),'Suspected bacterial meningitis in an immunocompetent adult under 50: get blood cultures, then LP (no CT indication here: no focal deficit, papilledema, altered consciousness or immunocompromise), and start IV ceftriaxone plus vancomycin; give dexamethasone with or before the first dose (IDSA 2004 bacterial meningitis guideline). Ampicillin-gentamicin is the neonatal regimen. Routine CT before LP delays care. Acyclovir alone with a delayed LP misses bacterial disease. Delaying antibiotics for MRI increases mortality.'),
('cmqgdhy66000q1yr8xjgscz3c','["Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and urgent surgical evaluation", "Topical antibiotic ointment only", "Oral cephalexin and wound care", "Observation with daily dressing changes"]'::jsonb,'["Daily dressing changes and recheck in 48 hours", "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and podiatry follow-up in 1 week", "Hyperbaric oxygen and outpatient oral antibiotics", "Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and urgent surgical evaluation", "IV vancomycin and MRI of the foot before surgical consult"]'::jsonb,'Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and urgent surgical evaluation','Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and urgent surgical evaluation','D',md5('A 55-year-old diabetic man has a warm, erythematous foot ulcer with purulent drainage. Temp 38.6°C, WBC 14.5 × 10³/µL, glucose 312 mg/dL. X-ray shows soft tissue gas.'),'A 55-year-old diabetic man has a warm, erythematous foot ulcer with purulent drainage. Temp 38.6°C, WBC 14.5 × 10³/µL, glucose 312 mg/dL. X-ray shows soft tissue gas.',md5('What is the most appropriate management?'),'What is the most appropriate management?',md5('Gas in soft tissues with systemic signs suggests necrotizing infection — requires IV antibiotics and surgical debridement. Topical or oral therapy alone is insufficient for limb-threatening infection.'),'Gas in soft tissues with systemic signs suggests necrotizing infection — requires IV antibiotics and surgical debridement. Topical or oral therapy alone is insufficient for limb-threatening infection.

Why not "Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and podiatry follow-up in 1 week": gas and SIRS signs mean severe infection needing IV therapy and surgery (IWGDF/IDSA 2023).
Why not "IV vancomycin and MRI of the foot before surgical consult": soft-tissue gas needs urgent surgical evaluation without imaging delay, and vancomycin alone is too narrow.
Why not "Hyperbaric oxygen and outpatient oral antibiotics": HBOT is an adjunct and has no role in place of debridement.
Why not "Daily dressing changes and recheck in 48 hours": this delays definitive treatment of a possible necrotizing infection.'),
('cmqgdhycy000s1yr8iosy3sbi','["Neurosurgical consultation and blood pressure control", "Lumbar puncture to confirm diagnosis", "Oral sumatriptan and discharge", "MRI brain in 2 weeks"]'::jsonb,'["Neurosurgical consultation and blood pressure control", "IV metoclopramide and repeat CT in 6 hours", "Lumbar puncture to confirm before neurosurgery referral", "Admit for observation and MRI brain in 48 hours", "IV heparin to prevent vasospasm and neurology consult"]'::jsonb,'Neurosurgical consultation and blood pressure control','Neurosurgical consultation and blood pressure control','A',md5('A 34-year-old woman presents with the worst headache of her life, sudden onset, associated with vomiting. BP 168/98 mm Hg, HR 104/min. Neck stiffness is present. CT head without contrast shows subarachnoid blood.'),'A 34-year-old woman presents with the worst headache of her life, sudden onset, associated with vomiting. BP 168/98 mm Hg, HR 104/min. Neck stiffness is present. CT head without contrast shows subarachnoid blood.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('CT-confirmed subarachnoid hemorrhage requires neurosurgical evaluation for aneurysm securing and BP management — LP is unnecessary when CT is positive. Triptans treat migraine, not SAH.'),'CT-confirmed subarachnoid hemorrhage requires neurosurgical evaluation for aneurysm securing and BP management — LP is unnecessary when CT is positive. Triptans treat migraine, not SAH.

Why not "Lumbar puncture to confirm before neurosurgery referral": CT already shows SAH, so LP is unnecessary (AHA/ASA aSAH 2023).
Why not "IV metoclopramide and repeat CT in 6 hours": it treats symptoms and delays securing the aneurysm.
Why not "Admit for observation and MRI brain in 48 hours": aSAH needs urgent aneurysm evaluation (CTA) and securing.
Why not "IV heparin to prevent vasospasm and neurology consult": anticoagulation is contraindicated; nimodipine is used for vasospasm.'),
('cmqgdhz7o00111yr88vhbbyxj','["Progestin-only method or nonhormonal IUD", "Combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive", "Transdermal estrogen patch", "Depo-medroxyprogesterone is absolutely contraindicated"]'::jsonb,'["Low-dose combined pill once she quits smoking", "Combined estrogen-progestin oral contraceptive", "Progestin-only method or nonhormonal IUD", "Combined hormonal patch with ethinyl estradiol", "Combined vaginal ring with ethinyl estradiol"]'::jsonb,'Progestin-only method or nonhormonal IUD','Progestin-only method or nonhormonal IUD','C',md5('A 17-year-old requests contraception. She has migraine with aura and smokes 10 cigarettes per day. BP 118/72 mm Hg, HR 76/min.'),'A 17-year-old requests contraception. She has migraine with aura and smokes 10 cigarettes per day. BP 118/72 mm Hg, HR 76/min.',md5('What is the most appropriate contraceptive recommendation?'),'What is the most appropriate contraceptive recommendation?',md5('Combined hormonal contraception is contraindicated with migraine with aura and smoking in women ≥35 (and caution in younger smokers) — progestin-only or copper IUD is safer. Depo-progestin is not absolutely contraindicated here.'),'Combined hormonal contraception is contraindicated with migraine with aura and smoking in women ≥35 (and caution in younger smokers) — progestin-only or copper IUD is safer. Depo-progestin is not absolutely contraindicated here.

Why not "Combined hormonal patch with ethinyl estradiol": migraine with aura is US MEC category 4 for every estrogen-containing method (CDC US MEC 2024).
Why not "Combined vaginal ring with ethinyl estradiol": same estrogen risk as the patch.
Why not "Low-dose combined pill once she quits smoking": migraine with aura alone is category 4, whether or not she smokes.'),
('cmqgqkr23001p1ydw46ub1lv0','["Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus", "Ketoacidosis"]'::jsonb,'["Type 1 diabetes mellitus", "Diabetes insipidus", "Maturity-onset diabetes of the young", "Stress hyperglycemia", "Type 2 diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb,'Type 1 diabetes mellitus','Type 1 diabetes mellitus','A',md5('A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric emergency department with a fever, irritability, and frequent urination. His mother reports that he has been drinking excessive amounts of water. His blood glucose level is found to be 350 mg/dL.'),'A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatric emergency department with 3 weeks of frequent urination, bedwetting, excessive thirst, and a 2-kg weight loss; today he has a low-grade fever and irritability. His blood glucose is 350 mg/dL, urine ketones are moderate, and venous pH is 7.36.',md5('What is the most likely diagnosis?'),'What is the most likely diagnosis?',md5('The clinical presentation of polyuria, polydipsia, and hyperglycemia in a child is characteristic of Type 1 diabetes mellitus, which is often diagnosed in this age group.

Clinical reasoning: The vignette suggests a classic presentation of Type 1 diabetes, making it the most likely diagnosis given the symptoms and lab findings.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Type 2 diabetes mellitus: This is less common in this age group and typically presents differently.
• Diabetes insipidus: This condition would present with polyuria but not necessarily with hyperglycemia.
• Ketoacidosis: While this can occur in Type 1 diabetes, it is not a diagnosis in itself but rather a complication.'),'Weeks of polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss, a glucose of 350 mg/dL and ketosis without acidosis in a young child are type 1 diabetes (ADA Standards of Care 2026, sections 2 and 14). Why not: stress hyperglycemia is transient and does not cause weeks of osmotic symptoms. T2DM and MODY are uncommon at age 4 and usually lack ketosis. DI causes polyuria without hyperglycemia.'),
('cmqgr7tqu000d1y8rtw9tw3is','["Administer activated charcoal", "Intubate the patient", "Administer flumazenil", "Start intravenous fluids"]'::jsonb,'["Urgent hemodialysis for drug removal", "Sodium bicarbonate IV bolus", "Flumazenil IV", "IV lipid emulsion therapy", "Physostigmine IV"]'::jsonb,'Intubate the patient','Sodium bicarbonate IV bolus','B',md5('A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency department after a suicide attempt by overdose of her antidepressant medication. She is unconscious and has a heart rate of 120 bpm, blood pressure of 90/60 mm Hg, and respiratory rate of 8 breaths per minute. Naloxone is administered, but there is no improvement.'),'A 40-year-old woman presents to the emergency department 4 hours after a suicide attempt by amitriptyline overdose. She was unconscious with a respiratory rate of 8 breaths per minute, did not respond to naloxone, and has been intubated; the airway is secured. HR 124 bpm, BP 86/54 mm Hg. ECG shows sinus tachycardia with a QRS of 132 ms.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Given the patient''s decreased level of consciousness and respiratory depression, intubation is necessary to secure the airway.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s vital signs indicate significant respiratory compromise, and securing the airway is the priority.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Administer activated charcoal: This is contraindicated in an unconscious patient due to the risk of aspiration.
• Administer flumazenil: Flumazenil is contraindicated in suspected poly-drug overdose as it can precipitate seizures.
• Start intravenous fluids: While IV fluids may be necessary later, the immediate priority is to secure the airway.'),'TCA cardiotoxicity (QRS over 100 ms and hypotension) is treated with an IV sodium bicarbonate bolus to alkalinize and load sodium (AHA 2023 focused update on ACLS for poisoning). Why not: TCAs are highly protein-bound with a large volume of distribution, so hemodialysis doesn''t remove them. Physostigmine can cause asystole in TCA overdose. Flumazenil lowers the seizure threshold. Lipid emulsion is a last-line rescue.'),
('cmqgdhw0r00051yr8ap2l03oh','["Spironolactone", "Amlodipine", "Hydralazine monotherapy", "Diltiazem"]'::jsonb,'["IV nitroprusside", "Amlodipine", "IV furosemide", "Spironolactone", "Oral furosemide 20 mg daily"]'::jsonb,'Spironolactone','IV furosemide','C',md5('A 71-year-old woman with HFrEF (EF 30%) on lisinopril and carvedilol presents with 3 days of worsening dyspnea and 2-pillow orthopnea. BP 108/72 mm Hg, HR 92/min, RR 24/min, SpO₂ 91% on room air. JVD to the angle of the jaw, bibasilar crackles, and 2+ pitting edema. Weight is up 4 kg from baseline.'),'A 71-year-old woman with HFrEF (EF 30%) on lisinopril and carvedilol presents with 3 days of worsening dyspnea and 2-pillow orthopnea. BP 108/72 mm Hg, HR 92/min, RR 24/min, SpO₂ 91% on room air. JVD to the angle of the jaw, bibasilar crackles, and 2+ pitting edema. Weight is up 4 kg from baseline.',md5('What is the most appropriate medication to add to her regimen?'),'What is the most appropriate initial medication?',md5('Guideline-directed medical therapy for HFrEF includes ACEi/ARB/ARNI, beta-blocker, and MRA when EF ≤35% without contraindications. She is already on ACEi and beta-blocker; spironolactone reduces mortality in symptomatic HFrEF. Amlodipine is not first-line for HFrEF. Non-dihydropyridine CCBs (diltiazem) are contraindicated in HFrEF. Hydralazine-isosorbide is an alternative when ACEi cannot be used, not before MRA.'),'Acute decompensated HFrEF with volume overload: prompt IV loop diuretic, at a dose of at least the home oral dose (2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF guideline, Class 1). Why not: low-dose oral furosemide is inadequate for decongestion because of gut edema. Spironolactone is GDMT to add later, not decongestion. Nitroprusside is unsafe at BP 108/72. Amlodipine has no HFrEF benefit.'),
('cmr81nhrt00131y0870t8lynn','["Acetaminophen", "Ceftriaxone", "Acyclovir", "Dexamethasone"]'::jsonb,'["Acetaminophen and observation", "Penicillin G and gentamicin", "Ampicillin, ceftriaxone and dexamethasone", "Acyclovir and dexamethasone", "Vancomycin, ceftriaxone and dexamethasone"]'::jsonb,'Ceftriaxone','Vancomycin, ceftriaxone and dexamethasone','E',md5('A 34-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. He reports that the headache started suddenly and has progressively worsened over the last few hours. On examination, his temperature is 39.2°C, and he exhibits a positive Brudzinski sign.'),'A 34-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe headache, photophobia, and neck stiffness. The headache began this morning and has progressively worsened over the last few hours. On examination, his temperature is 39.2°C, and he exhibits a positive Brudzinski sign. He is immunocompetent, and blood cultures have been drawn.',md5('What is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?'),'What is the most appropriate empiric pharmacotherapy?',md5('The correct answer is Ceftriaxone, as this broad-spectrum antibiotic is the first-line treatment for suspected bacterial meningitis, which is indicated by the patient''s symptoms of severe headache, fever, and neck stiffness. Option Acetaminophen is incorrect because it does not address the underlying infection. Option Acyclovir is incorrect unless viral meningitis or herpes simplex is suspected, which is not indicated in this case. Option Dexamethasone is incorrect as it is used as an adjunctive therapy in bacterial meningitis but not as the initial treatment.'),'Empiric therapy for suspected bacterial meningitis in an immunocompetent adult under 50 is vancomycin plus a third-generation cephalosporin, with dexamethasone given before or with the first dose (IDSA 2004, archived and still IDSA''s most recent; ESCMID 2016; WHO 2025 meningitis guideline). Why not: ampicillin for Listeria is added only at age 50 or over or with immunocompromise. Acyclovir is for HSV encephalitis. Penicillin plus gentamicin is not empiric coverage for resistant pneumococcus. Observation delays treatment.'),
('cmqgqv43s001i1y4vhxau2rql','["Start a daily inhaled corticosteroid", "Increase the dose of albuterol", "Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist", "Refer to a pulmonologist"]'::jsonb,'["Budesonide-formoterol maintenance and reliever", "Add salmeterol with as-needed albuterol", "Refer to a pulmonologist", "Oral montelukast nightly and albuterol as needed", "Increase the dose of albuterol"]'::jsonb,'Start a daily inhaled corticosteroid','Budesonide-formoterol maintenance and reliever','A',md5('A 35-year-old woman with a known history of asthma presents to the clinic for follow-up. She reports increased shortness of breath and wheezing, especially at night. Her current medication regimen includes a rescue inhaler (albuterol) but no daily controller medication. On examination, her peak flow is 60% of predicted.'),'A 35-year-old woman with a known history of asthma presents to the clinic for follow-up. Over the past month she has used albuterol 4 to 5 days per week and wakes at night with wheezing about twice a week. Her current medication regimen includes a rescue inhaler (albuterol) but no daily controller medication. On examination, her peak flow is 60% of predicted.',md5('What is the most appropriate next step in management?'),'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',md5('Inhaled corticosteroids are the first-line treatment for persistent asthma and are essential for controlling inflammation and preventing exacerbations.

Clinical reasoning: The patient''s symptoms indicate poorly controlled asthma, and initiating a daily controller medication will help improve her lung function and reduce nighttime symptoms.

Why other options are incorrect:
• Increase the dose of albuterol: Increasing the dose of a rescue inhaler is not appropriate as it does not address the underlying inflammation and can lead to over-reliance on rescue medication.
• Prescribe a long-acting beta-agonist: While LABAs are used for asthma, they should be used in conjunction with inhaled corticosteroids, not as monotherapy.
• Refer to a pulmonologist: Referral is not necessary at this stage; management can be effectively handled in primary care with appropriate medication adjustments.'),'GINA 2025 Track 1 (preferred): with night waking and reduced PEF on SABA, start low-dose budesonide-formoterol as maintenance-and-reliever therapy, and stop SABA-only treatment. Why not: an LTRA is a less effective controller (FDA boxed warning for neuropsychiatric effects). A LABA without ICS increases asthma death risk. More albuterol worsens outcomes. Referral is not needed at this step.'),
('cmqgdhzrw00171yr85kotzuvn','["Plasma free metanephrines", "TSH", "Random cortisol", "Hemoglobin A1c"]'::jsonb,'["TSH", "Plasma free metanephrines", "CT of the adrenal glands", "Random cortisol", "Plasma aldosterone-renin ratio"]'::jsonb,'Plasma free metanephrines','Plasma free metanephrines','B',md5('A 38-year-old man presents with sudden severe headache, diaphoresis, and BP 210/118 mm Hg. HR 124/min. After IV labetalol, BP remains 195/110 mm Hg with ongoing symptoms.'),'A 38-year-old man presents with sudden severe headache, diaphoresis, and BP 210/118 mm Hg. HR 124/min. He reports 3 similar episodes of headache, palpitations and sweating in the past month.',md5('What is the most appropriate diagnostic test?'),'What is the most appropriate diagnostic test?',md5('Episodic hypertension with headache and tachycardia suggests pheochromocytoma — plasma or urine metanephrines are the screening test. TSH, cortisol, and A1c do not evaluate catecholamine excess.'),'Paroxysmal headache, diaphoresis, tachycardia and severe HTN suggest pheochromocytoma. Biochemical testing with plasma free (or 24-h urine fractionated) metanephrines comes first (Endocrine Society 2014 pheochromocytoma/paraganglioma guideline). Safety: alpha blockade (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) must come before any beta-blocker, including labetalol, which is mainly beta-blocking. Unopposed alpha stimulation can precipitate hypertensive crisis. Why not: imaging comes only after biochemical confirmation. Aldosteronism doesn''t cause adrenergic spells. TSH and cortisol don''t fit.');
